"field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374720} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6903a81-a062-51dc-8e00-e0bf9cf8ba8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374725} {:answer/id #uuid "daa492b5-baae-551d-914f-9bf6ff082357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374726} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ba00b8-d3f4-543c-93d0-c999cc5daea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374727}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374725} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b403dbf-11be-5e50-b26f-8a6414514eb6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b4fe65a-de2c-554e-ac09-a9f3be4dfb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374729} {:answer/id #uuid "a319e619-9a7d-588b-91a6-bac1a130ba1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374730} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fb30a9-0649-5677-9846-52c4b215650f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374731}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374731} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b05e4c-7200-55b4-96ba-ad42a7e6255e" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374728} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcdf2394-d124-52a7-86fb-09532a5e3859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374733} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c8ef7c-9918-5483-97c3-53f92fb62444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374734} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3887c1-6c24-52e6-9108-0da137d4e21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374735}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374734} :answer-field/id #uuid "b11f479d-c44c-5425-a34e-34dbc282f015" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "142f96c0-88b4-5504-b457-f38e843c5844" :question/math-academy-id "q-333380" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x(2y-x-1),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{11}{12}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n  {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{11}{12}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x(2y-x-1) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot \\left(2\\cdot \\frac{11}{12}-1-1\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{11}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=0.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}-0\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{11}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{11}{12}+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{11}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =-{x}_{\\text{new}}(2{y}_{p}-{x}_{\\text{new}}-1) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\left(2\\cdot \\frac{7}{6}-2-1\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{11}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\left(5\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}-0\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{11}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{11}{12}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{19}{12}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{11}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{19}{12}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{19}{12}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2076f2cd-c543-56b6-ad48-12dd8fde3c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{6}{800-4t}Q=150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374782} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8c0df8-8e84-5b4a-a537-e767ba5bd106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{10}{800-4t}Q=150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374783} {:answer/id #uuid "2aaf35ea-5d23-586b-a537-ad4746bbf44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{6}{800-4t}Q=150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374784} {:answer/id #uuid "3890801d-4580-51ee-a29c-8a62016a2731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{10}{800-4t}Q=25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374785} {:answer/id #uuid "bae663a2-dc22-5cb3-8929-a5b72e73eb04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{10}{800-4t}Q=150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374786}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374786} :answer-field/id #uuid "695f8029-277d-5256-9666-b6d4c7209899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9676c54d-7964-5ac5-b29f-e86e6a304d79" :question/math-academy-id "q-336493" :question/problem "An irrigation tank initially holds $800\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $1\\,200\\,\\text{g}$ of dissolved fertilizer.\nA solution containing $25\\,\\text{g/L}$ of dissolved fertilizer is pumped into the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of fertilizer $Q$ in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the mass of fertilizer in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $25\\,\\text{g/L}$ of fertilizer enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *fertilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(25\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(25\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =150\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $800\\,\\text{L}$ of water.\n\n- A solution enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $6t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed water solution leaves the tank at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $10t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n800+6t-10t=800-4t\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank, the *concentration* of the water solution at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{800-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *fertilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{800-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{800-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{800-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =150-\\frac{10Q}{800-4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{10}{800-4t}Q=150.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65953d1e-f31e-5b66-a6e3-2c4788a9b56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{4}{100-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374789} {:answer/id #uuid "b7056eb9-6f0e-56ef-a4f3-a7a23d8d7bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374790} {:answer/id #uuid "986792c6-c427-575c-bbf7-569ea23446d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{100-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374791} {:answer/id #uuid "51ac428d-f3ca-5b7b-b5cb-7847f2ae941d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{3}{100-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374792} {:answer/id #uuid "89926de3-6bc5-5c70-be46-1fee6eff8c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{100-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374791} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc3221e6-b399-5c13-bbef-bd6d45c5c69b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a6f7f83-92ac-5a59-9bf9-fbcfeba64934" :question/math-academy-id "q-336556" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $12\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt.\nA brine solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt per liter is poured into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of salt $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the brine begins to be added?" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Brine enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $15t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+15t-20t=(500-5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{500-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{500-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{500-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{100-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{4Q}{100-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{100-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b531876e-771f-5aa7-828f-2c8d9e3d6412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}Q=2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374808} {:answer/id #uuid "f34c8ff4-74e5-50eb-94a9-562be3772608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{1}{30}Q=2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374809} {:answer/id #uuid "df968ca3-8717-5887-a909-81c5c0feba48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{1}{40}Q=2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374810} {:answer/id #uuid "edb2e738-857b-593a-8d1b-764aef1c4cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}Q=2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374811} {:answer/id #uuid "0763b262-b396-5099-8228-b56d41d5c4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}Q=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374812}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374811} :answer-field/id #uuid "6355f015-b4aa-504a-a8b4-2f8218939bf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "810434a6-a935-5080-a9b1-d83eec744584" :question/math-academy-id "q-336619" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $540\\,\\text{L}$ of a chemical solution containing $22\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved chemical.\nA chemical solution containing $0.15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved chemical per liter is poured into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of dissolved chemical $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added?" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dissolved chemical in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a chemical solution containing $0.15\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved chemical enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved chemical* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.15\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.15\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =2.7\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $540\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Chemical solution enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n540+18t-18t=540\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved chemical in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{540}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dissolved chemical* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{540}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{540}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{18Q}{540}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{30}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.7-\\frac{Q}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}Q=2.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bc23275-f819-5950-a397-14085fd246fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{9}{160}Q=2.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374815} {:answer/id #uuid "b82d70e4-4fa1-5fa8-8b3a-5a4177968985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{9}{160}Q=2.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374816} {:answer/id #uuid "36068162-a5d4-50d7-ba5a-10becb446657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=2.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374817} {:answer/id #uuid "184780ab-5408-53d3-bfcc-3062212d12d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{9}{160}Q=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374818} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9a3c73-9502-519b-8b12-858c0935508f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{1}{20}Q=2.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374815} :answer-field/id #uuid "390fb85f-a87d-58b5-86e4-38ca65f5a5bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "853d12b2-cf0a-55d3-82af-8fb0a9992819" :question/math-academy-id "q-336621" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $320\\,\\text{L}$ of a chemical solution containing $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved chemical.\nA chemical solution containing $0.14\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved chemical per liter is poured into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of dissolved chemical $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added?" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dissolved chemical in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a chemical solution containing $0.14\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved chemical enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved chemical* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.14\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.14\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =2.52\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $320\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Chemical solution enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n320+18t-18t=320\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved chemical in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{320}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dissolved chemical* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{320}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{320}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{18Q}{320}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9Q}{160}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.52-\\frac{9Q}{160}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{9}{160}Q=2.52.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374820 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "835ed941-71af-508b-b962-5bfd810b7918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374822} {:answer/id #uuid "0468a81b-2239-5f53-84a9-7481ee0cfb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18e^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501475} :answer-field/id #uuid "17c7259c-bd73-5489-8857-2aa304112761" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bc17f053-4154-5b58-a673-a3b7c9595b38" :question/math-academy-id "q-336630" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $260\\,\\text{L}$ of a dye solution containing $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye.\nPure water is poured into the tank at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind an equation for the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the pure water is first added.\n\n$Q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that pure water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dye) enters the tank at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(13\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(13\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nNote that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume of solution in the tank remains $260\\,\\text{L}.$\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(13\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(13\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{13Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{Q}{20} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{20} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{20}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{20}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-t/20+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/20},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20} & =18 \\\\ A & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=18{e}^{-t/20}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374823 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bf9c62e-c4f9-5e3c-8ed7-61d9f3e8d86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{e}^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374825}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374825} :answer-field/id #uuid "28133954-7d6d-50c3-9942-b8e9c9a1182b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d568f5f-6232-5ee8-a10c-db439f4a3317" :question/math-academy-id "q-336631" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $375\\,\\text{L}$ of a dissolved contaminant solution containing $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant.\nPure water is poured into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind an equation for the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the pure water is first added.\n\n$Q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dissolved contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that pure water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant exits the tank.\n\nNote that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume of solution in the tank remains $375\\,\\text{L}.$\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of dissolved contaminant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{375}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dissolved contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{375}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{375}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{375}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{Q}{25} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{25}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{25}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{25}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-t/25+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/25},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/25} & =9 \\\\ A & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=9{e}^{-t/25}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "587ad7eb-e7ff-5449-a3e7-315421a5c0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=20{\\left(\\frac{100}{100+t}\\right)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374828} {:answer/id #uuid "95d74708-bfdc-53f7-9971-439077d9c125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=2500{\\left(\\frac{10}{100+t}\\right)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374829} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ca561d-2102-5596-b774-af92e2184dde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=25\\frac{100}{(100+t)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374830} {:answer/id #uuid "28f84566-0022-5a52-aa79-a865a20593a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=25{\\left(\\frac{100}{100+t}\\right)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374831} {:answer/id #uuid "64fc0482-ab86-5439-a2a9-8a2e97fe0fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=20{\\left(\\frac{10}{100+t}\\right)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374831} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5507560-e644-5b06-9ffc-b163d1f6589b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3c2a5b4-3b5e-50f6-abee-7b39dd34294c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336632" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $25\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind an equation for the amount of salt $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water is first added." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Fresh water enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $12t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+18t-12t=(600+6t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{100+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{2Q}{100+t} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{100+t}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{100+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{100+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{100+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(100+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $25\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(100+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ A & =25\\cdot {100}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=25{\\left(\\frac{100}{100+t}\\right)}^{2}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3bcae1d-4628-5d5e-9866-09a9d7efbdd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{120-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374835} {:answer/id #uuid "806caa57-314d-5681-a084-5c14533971ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{120-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374836} {:answer/id #uuid "6321aa1d-de14-58db-b640-d13ad117e1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{3}{120-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374837} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb2afc2-73fa-5451-81b5-589eac362299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}-\\frac{4}{120-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374838} {:answer/id #uuid "297a00d2-6abb-5d7d-bb3c-fa87b11ab5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{120-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374836} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb4e1568-32ff-52b1-93ad-532a11c031d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bc940784-de54-59ae-ac63-40781cc5a4ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-336666" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{gal}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt.\nA brine solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt per gallon flows into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of salt $Q,$ in pounds, in the tank $t$ minutes after the inflow begins?" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nThe inflowing brine has concentration $0.02\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ and flows into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal}/\\text{min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine.\n\n- Brine enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $15t\\,\\text{gal}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{gal}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+15t-20t=(600-5t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{600-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{600-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{600-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{120-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{4Q}{120-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{120-t}Q=\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22446d9b-f3b3-51d7-8eab-f813f126f966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.43\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374842} {:answer/id #uuid "fc791120-4af1-5df5-8a8b-8cf249933b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7.54\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374843} {:answer/id #uuid "2a979bc1-8055-5284-be10-31b92bc2fb71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.22\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374844} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ab7b77-90c7-560a-a520-39ae98136e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.08\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374845} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb6f7cb-44c0-5462-90c0-5e816014480d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.70\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374846}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374842} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9b1d72-7dcf-5756-8025-f1f5167127d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a7733334-ec35-5062-9a3d-109d06df1cd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336674" :question/problem "A wastewater holding basin initially holds $480\\,\\text{gal}$ of water containing $12\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminants.\nClean water is pumped into the basin at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid leaves the basin at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminants in the basin $10$ minutes after the clean water pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminants in the basin at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminants enter the basin minus the rate at which contaminants leave the basin.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminants enter the basin.\n\nNote that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminants) enters the basin at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminants* enter the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminants exit the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminants leave the basin, we first determine the volume of wastewater in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $480\\,\\text{gal}$ of wastewater; water enters at $24\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and drains out at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of wastewater in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n480+24t-18t=(480+6t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminants in the basin, the *concentration* of the wastewater at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminants* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{18Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{80+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3Q}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{3}{80+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{3}{80+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{3}{80+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{80+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(80+t)^{3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $12\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminants. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=12,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =12 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(80+0)^{3}} & =12 \\\\ A & =12\\cdot {80}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12{\\left(\\frac{80}{80+t}\\right)}^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminants in the basin $10$ minutes after the clean water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(12)=12{\\left(\\frac{80}{90}\\right)}^{3}≈8.43\\,\\text{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "330391ea-80fb-555c-8100-9cf56bac72be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374849} {:answer/id #uuid "5345a1b0-bd58-534b-b7e1-91989f0e42ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374850} {:answer/id #uuid "ec22cd8b-9dd7-517c-8622-92daa295ef50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374851} {:answer/id #uuid "b3caacbd-86cd-5fc7-86f1-4bebadf1735e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374852} {:answer/id #uuid "47645fa8-cc54-5919-9b81-fbaad91413e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374852} :answer-field/id #uuid "94ee6f2e-5f4c-5a4b-80d2-dd6fa77ca97a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dcfe7bdc-5ce2-5316-9de6-7dc380d1901a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336726" :question/problem "A $240\\,\\text{gal}$-capacity tank initially holds $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $32\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. Fresh water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $3\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine flows out at a rate of $2\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Find the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $3\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; fresh water enters at $3\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed brine leaves at $2\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=150+3t-2t=(150+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{150+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $2\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(2\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{150+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{150+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{150+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{150+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{150+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{150+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{150+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{150+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|150+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(150+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(150+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(150+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $32\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=32,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(150+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ A & =32(150)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{32(150)^{2}}{(150+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =32{\\left(\\frac{150}{150+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $240\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=240$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =240 \\\\ 150+{t}_{o} & =240 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =90\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(90) & =32{\\left(\\frac{150}{150+90}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =32{\\left(\\frac{150}{240}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =32{\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =12.5\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d90ed3-593b-56eb-b15c-74fb96a264c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374856} {:answer/id #uuid "23a41576-4255-58aa-aff3-24970b0750f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374857} {:answer/id #uuid "162b2841-c921-5383-b411-667946024eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "55\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374858} {:answer/id #uuid "a400a2ae-64d3-5df6-8b72-04dc312a8573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374859} {:answer/id #uuid "2c007732-c2d9-5745-a9ef-073e3655a986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374860}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374857} :answer-field/id #uuid "86cb9083-a221-51ed-ad2c-ae6d13e72d9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45ce4d33-a7f8-5fd0-bbf2-c53f0966bb82" :question/math-academy-id "q-336727" :question/problem "A $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity reactor initially holds $7\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of a chemical solution containing $98\\,\\text{g}$ of reactant. Pure solvent begins to flow into the reactor at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed solution flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $7\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of solution; pure solvent enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed solution leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reactor after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,500+90t-60t=30(250+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reactor is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{250+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{250+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{250+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{250+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{250+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{250+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|250+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(250+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(250+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(250+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $98\\,\\text{g}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=98,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =98 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(250+0)^{2}} & =98 \\\\ A & =98(250)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{98(250)^{2}}{(250+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =98{\\left(\\frac{250}{250+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reactor begins to overflow. The reactor has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(250+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 250+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =98{\\left(\\frac{250}{250+100}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =98{\\left(\\frac{250}{350}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =98{\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =50\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cb4e1cb-a4d9-5a79-8bad-83176cb4a640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.60\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374863} {:answer/id #uuid "97b47761-bf18-5c2c-807b-5fbd6eecd397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.85\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374864} {:answer/id #uuid "f9316b8e-3e20-5a4c-8d57-cf7e0b5d688e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.46\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374865} {:answer/id #uuid "8dde20d6-6676-5b10-94da-9eee8eb3183f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.19\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374866} {:answer/id #uuid "07f0a2a1-f819-5785-94c7-449359e5a659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.02\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374867} :answer-field/id #uuid "617c7a09-689e-563c-b4f9-daf6d3871ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a99a5d4e-3d03-5070-bc7f-206f5695b61b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336822" :question/problem "A $310\\,\\text{gal}$-capacity reactor initially holds $190\\,\\text{gal}$ of a chemical solution containing $32\\,\\text{lb}$ of reactant. Pure solvent begins to flow into the reactor at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution flows out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $190\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; pure solvent enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed solution leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=190+6t-4t=2(95+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reactor is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{95+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{95+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{95+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{4}{190+2t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{4}{190+2t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{95+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|95+t|\\right)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|95+t|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(95+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(95+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(95+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $32\\,\\text{lb}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=32,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(95+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ A & =32(95)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{32(190)^{2}}{(95+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =32{\\left(\\frac{95}{95+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reactor begins to overflow. The reactor has a capacity of $310\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=310$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =310 \\\\ 190+2{t}_{o} & =310 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =32{\\left(\\frac{95}{95+60}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =32{\\left(\\frac{95}{155}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈12.02\\,\\text{lb},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89eecff6-8175-550d-ad43-2b9766bd7fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "67.49\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374870} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed6a48d-1428-52d9-a522-2bee31663016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "65.75\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374871} {:answer/id #uuid "17ca700c-b4a3-5002-abdc-7f9a8e703bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "63.98\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374872} {:answer/id #uuid "51f09e87-40c3-5d6f-9b67-9acbd58b27a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70.10\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374873} {:answer/id #uuid "5118b033-cfac-501e-bbca-b3f1d3ca44b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "61.42\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374874}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374871} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfba8ca-3fd1-51fd-9b05-5695d3defec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "98bdf48f-bd8c-581b-9efb-c84bc03b4bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-336826" :question/problem "A $9\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity vat initially holds $6\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of a sugar-water solution containing $300\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. Pure water begins to flow into the vat at a rate of $100\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed sugar-water flows out at a rate of $80\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of sugar in the vat at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that pure water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of sugar) enters the vat at a rate of $100\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(100\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(100\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of sugar-water in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $6\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of solution; pure water enters at $100\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed solution leaves at $80\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the vat after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=6\\,500+100t-80t=20(325+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the vat is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $80\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(80\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(80\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{325+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{4Q}{325+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{4}{325+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{4}{325+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{4}{325+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{325+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|325+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(325+t)^{-4}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(325+t)^{-4}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(325+t)^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $300\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=300,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =300 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(325+0)^{4}} & =300 \\\\ A & =300(325)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{300(325)^{4}}{(325+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =300{\\left(\\frac{325}{325+t}\\right)}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the vat begins to overflow. The vat has a capacity of $9\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=9\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =9\\,500 \\\\ 20(325+{t}_{o}) & =9\\,500 \\\\ 325+{t}_{o} & =475 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =150\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of sugar in the vat at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(150) & =300{\\left(\\frac{325}{325+150}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =300{\\left(\\frac{13}{19}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈65.75\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a615dd0-3f77-5dfd-b26b-39f0cde70998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge -5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374995} {:answer/id #uuid "8f291ac4-6543-5679-93d1-65e628f4e1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374996} {:answer/id #uuid "c31043e3-2b67-5c50-b765-9054581ce030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374997} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ff7972-c57c-554b-8941-adb57964773a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374998} {:answer/id #uuid "19bc009f-a48d-5f7c-919b-2e83f1d36c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374999} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6bd806-3f16-506f-beea-59f026bce5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375000}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374996} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ebbcb18-2090-5c68-b6a4-04c751298594" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c52f968-3e9b-592a-b3ef-f342903dab7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375002} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4f5c6c-e882-5e0f-897e-2e17ff4bda27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5s}{{s}^{2}-25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375003} {:answer/id #uuid "890e4ab9-4f40-5bf0-bb86-49177abaae90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375004} {:answer/id #uuid "e0bb0e97-035a-5499-84fc-66ceeac6ae0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}-25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375005} {:answer/id #uuid "138765c8-390e-53dc-a580-7516a0dc580b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375006}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375005} :answer-field/id #uuid "7955e3ad-1f9d-50d6-9e96-71c3d2b43bbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3dbc92cb-1339-57fe-b1ef-0f6893134b4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-341125" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)=\\frac{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-5t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5t}}{2}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-5t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{5t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-5t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5},\\,s>5 \\\\ L{{e}^{-5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+5},\\,s>-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{5t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-5t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{s+5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{(s+5)-(s-5)}{(s-5)(s+5)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}-25}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}-25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-5,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(5,\\infty )\\cap (-5,\\infty )=(5,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b5eb1c8-01c9-5671-a035-b45ec2547eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375030}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375030} :answer-field/id #uuid "c83b4c80-e4d4-5493-b84f-95e022ee40b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "010f8799-9b1d-5084-ad88-8118957f7d57" :question/math-academy-id "q-343349" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e41308fb734cdb42fe0d199a46e7db8edd25bd5d9ea5a3fabff0601a42dce51.png)\n\nA rectangular loaf of rising bread dough is squeezed between two rigid side panels that keep its width fixed at $60\\,\\text{mm}.$ The length of the loaf is decreasing by $2\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}$ and its height is increasing by $1\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is its volume changing when its dimensions are $100\\,\\text{mm}$ in length and $30\\,\\text{mm}$ in height?\n\nThe volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L,$ $W,$ and $H$ be the length, width, and height of the bread loaf, respectively, and\n\n- let $V$ be the volume of the bread loaf.\n\nNote that $L,$ $H,$ and $V$ change with time, while $W$ is constant. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=100\\text{ mm},\\, & W=60\\text{ mm}, \\\\ H=30\\text{ mm},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ mm/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=1\\text{ mm/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the three time-dependent variables, $V,$ $H$ and $L,$ is given by the formula for the volume of bread loaf:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating $W$ as a constant, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\left[L\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+H\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}\\right)\\right] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot L\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}\\right)+W\\cdot H\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(60)(100)(1)+(60)(30)(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =6000-3600 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =2400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of bread loaf is increasing at a rate of $2400\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dcce98c-8760-56f2-b17b-5fd945b150e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375033}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375033} :answer-field/id #uuid "948a4c6b-6866-5b53-840b-620e511c4ece" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ae903844-3154-5fe9-a420-6a9e8272ce91" :question/math-academy-id "q-343366" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-26, & x\\in [-5,0) \\\\ -3x, & x\\in [0,5)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=5.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=5.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(5) & =\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)=-3(5)=-15 \\\\ {f}^{+}(5) & =\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={5}^{2}-26=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(5)+{f}^{+}(5)}{2}=\\frac{-15+(-1)}{2}=-8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb5a35bc-6e98-5466-b248-220b58805210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375036}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375036} :answer-field/id #uuid "73e9342f-7d4b-572d-ae06-c85b2c42d4ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8508b9f1-644b-5b60-9272-323a48701a7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343367" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{0+2}{2}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2acba6b-85a0-5271-998e-5cb3d652c89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375057} :answer-field/id #uuid "05225483-6672-597f-a26b-7bc809c08b7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a441f1e0-bba8-5858-84bf-38b79256c3c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343451" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x\\right).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=2 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{f}^{+}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{2}=\\frac{2+(-4)}{2}=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6658038-57c3-5b1e-a0e8-5c2429b7e472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375063} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fc5eb32-bd14-5550-b830-a9aef3837e01" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f58af2a1-073c-5f4c-87d5-1658e07ab17d" :question/math-academy-id "q-346866" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\frac{3}{2}(0)+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e43e2c3-5048-5e2e-a94a-bcb90d586202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-{u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375105} {:answer/id #uuid "b13b11c1-2465-561f-a96b-19fa507beb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375106} {:answer/id #uuid "f152898a-1a3f-57ba-8463-c69b4da0c17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-{v}^{2}+3{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375107} {:answer/id #uuid "3b55205c-68ca-554b-93eb-3fc21cfe0e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-2{v}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375108} {:answer/id #uuid "11837235-5be9-5816-bdf3-58b69896ee4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-5{v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375109}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375106} :answer-field/id #uuid "227ed5b3-0cfb-51b9-820b-b40696387d27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db1fee9d-f261-54f6-9563-ad9a8f609d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38369" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le 2x+y\\le 5,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2x+y$ and $v=y,$ the integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u-v}{2},\\,y=v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}\\left[2\\cdot \\frac{u-v}{2}\\cdot v+{\\left(\\frac{u-v}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\right]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\left(uv-{v}^{2}+\\frac{{u}^{2}-2uv+{v}^{2}}{4}\\right)\\frac{1}{2}\\,du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4da866c3-3d7f-5b0b-a810-ac33ce6a862f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\ln }^{2}⁡y=(1-x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375169} {:answer/id #uuid "ff36e7bd-a5e4-5f39-b596-7ff7d49622b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\ln }^{2}⁡y=1-2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375170} {:answer/id #uuid "b064fdd0-a36c-5cee-92e7-9e10247d699e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\ln }^{2}⁡y=1-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375171} {:answer/id #uuid "7c31dc57-4c05-53a8-8a8e-94c43bdec15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\ln }^{2}⁡y={x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375172} {:answer/id #uuid "86e781a0-f04d-53d1-ab6b-bd9ecd3bb5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\ln }^{2}⁡y=2{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375171} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c617fb-7a13-5c0b-b392-e9e8047bec0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0ff30466-1dca-57e2-9c20-c453c1fbb920" :question/math-academy-id "q-50219" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{xy}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\n$$ that satisfies $y(0)=e.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{xy}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\text{d}y & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the integral in the left-hand side of this equality, we use a substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Then,  we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=\\frac{1}{y}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nSo we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\right) \\\\  & =\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\ln }^{2}⁡y+{C}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\ln }^{2}⁡y+{C}_{2} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {\\ln }^{2}⁡y & =-{x}^{2}+2({C}_{1}-{C}_{2}) \\\\ {\\ln }^{2}⁡y & =C-{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2({C}_{1}-{C}_{2})$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=e$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\ln }^{2}⁡e & =C-{0}^{2} \\\\ {1}^{2} & =C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n{\\ln }^{2}⁡y=1-{x}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "422a9438-abe1-5c55-9ce6-48992d025b3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375216} {:answer/id #uuid "55b2ef29-4c64-5208-b231-493dd0d99e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375217} {:answer/id #uuid "d38953c0-d8ac-5f78-b9aa-bbe42ba7fcb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375218} {:answer/id #uuid "16513a0b-e2a2-51c9-93af-f7d244d5921b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375219} {:answer/id #uuid "b581dad9-71c8-5464-8ccf-339c4aad013e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{63}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375219} :answer-field/id #uuid "36625350-cf95-5b46-804d-7df1ad8a967c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eea9417e-29cb-566f-8b4b-965dab594289" :question/math-academy-id "q-67141" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=1,$ $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the above formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}} & =1\\cdot \\frac{\\left[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{6}\\right]}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{63}{64}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad41d651-3be0-5db6-8319-017843ca96c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23.972" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375223} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8f684a-6570-5f41-9112-e0a0d2d8edf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35.951" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375224} {:answer/id #uuid "b611b28a-b3d5-586d-ae2a-0ddfb4db0a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17.988" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375225} {:answer/id #uuid "41b32af4-5012-5aa5-98f4-d2eb91bbcb40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17.049" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375226} {:answer/id #uuid "016c6493-7992-5112-b334-33c9029f3d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24.030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375224} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f3d7078-bb33-5108-97c3-04fe446f1d0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8fd9dda9-8f9c-5be8-b9a2-64b3fd691302" :question/math-academy-id "q-67149" :question/problem "Determine the value of the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{5}8{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}\n$$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Also, note that $N=6,$ since there are $6$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=8{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =8{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{0-1} \\\\  & =8{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =8\\left(\\frac{3}{1}\\right) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=24,r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $N=6$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{6} & =24\\cdot \\frac{\\left[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{6}\\right]}{\\left(1-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =24\\cdot \\frac{\\left[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{6}\\right]}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =36\\cdot \\left(\\frac{728}{729}\\right) \\\\  & ≈35.951\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e74e64f3-951a-56f6-942b-1a8b1e4d9ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375242} {:answer/id #uuid "718d61ae-eb0f-5e17-9838-c30510f38774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375243} {:answer/id #uuid "34a78b9a-d45a-5d3a-afae-f5256d6c28d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375244} {:answer/id #uuid "bd479ca7-9b45-5b70-8c35-7d3d5868fb0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{49}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375245} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c920ca-c80b-53f5-b4d8-a691b2d66eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{343}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375246}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375246} :answer-field/id #uuid "332e93fa-094d-5b37-a81d-6792b8481d65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0d02ca8e-0073-596b-95a3-b3760537618d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67216" :question/problem "If $k\\left(1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{13}\\right)$ is the sum of the first $13$ terms of the geometric series $49-7+1-⋯,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nIn our case, ${a}_{1}=49.$ To find the value of $r,$ we find the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-7}{49}=-\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=49,N=13$ and $r=-\\frac{1}{7}$ into our formula, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{13} & =49\\cdot \\frac{\\left(1-{\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{13}\\right)}{\\left(1-\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\right)} \\\\  & =49\\cdot \\frac{\\left(1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{13}\\right)}{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{7}\\right)} \\\\  & =49\\cdot \\frac{\\left(1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{13}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)}\\cdot \\left(1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{13}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{343}{8}\\cdot \\left(1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{13}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the expression above with $k\\left(1+{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{13}\\right),$ we obtain $k=\\frac{343}{8}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c7787a-31a6-57b0-b969-bd5a3135ac96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375344} {:answer/id #uuid "ea54053a-6425-5e9e-9a77-4524ffd6d1a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375345} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d2f994-5b9c-5919-a4e1-d619a8e6da32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\ln }^{2}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375346} {:answer/id #uuid "1c809f7d-d7ef-5008-a293-5216d4ae0256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375347} {:answer/id #uuid "e0158386-27f6-5854-8b7c-bf137f533a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\ln }^{2}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375345} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf307e2-aa61-5c5c-9a35-cb1835a62040" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1db700ab-3b05-5d1d-a411-1cbed80c9e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73657" :question/problem "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{6{\\log }_{8}⁡(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={\\log }_{8}⁡(2x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\cdot 2=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡8\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1/3$ | $2/3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{6{\\log }_{8}⁡(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\log }_{8}⁡(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1/3}^{2/3}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡8{\\int }_{1/3}^{2/3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡8\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1/3}^{2/3} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡8\\cdot \\left(\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{1}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44e18b50-5a34-5995-980b-2b315b3a6f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375518} {:answer/id #uuid "594f9a4a-d6e9-5df8-a308-2eb4cbda2ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375519} {:answer/id #uuid "01cb4d30-859b-537e-bd09-5b716f143dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375520} {:answer/id #uuid "047a39d6-26a5-5925-9b60-74ca9ec1ef3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375521} {:answer/id #uuid "88731088-8329-5a12-8959-6718187bcd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375521} :answer-field/id #uuid "83dc21d0-96ad-5f47-bb56-3352df831f55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "03366686-a1a7-5aa1-bce5-fc561e685ebd" :question/math-academy-id "q-83651" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le x\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}|x+y|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x+y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ} & =\\{(u,v)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le u\\le 3,\\hspace{1em}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v{\\left[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{1}^{3}dv \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a86f489c-529f-5017-8924-f7c173792d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375532} {:answer/id #uuid "eba9b63d-1a4e-509c-a1ac-1ca45f060494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375533} {:answer/id #uuid "7d29b823-4ba4-57c7-a665-6147cc81192a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-2t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375534} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3799a1-7a4b-530f-971d-91a1181804d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375535} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5f4b66-c313-52cb-a50a-70561713bb63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375536}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375535} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a2c2e46-526b-5812-b102-34de4caa62b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "02b3d021-7593-5ac9-9257-81d284bac79e" :question/math-academy-id "q-84247" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},$ where $t\\ge 1$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=4$ and velocity $v=0$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{1}{1}+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=-\\frac{1}{t}+1.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{t}+1\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1+K \\\\ 4 & =-0+1+K \\\\ K & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t+3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b587af18-df81-5db4-88ae-e75eb49804be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375626} {:answer/id #uuid "42dbc938-1217-5910-be74-c7acaaa09ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375627} {:answer/id #uuid "d880a9fe-7ede-58b5-95dc-503280226675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375628} {:answer/id #uuid "c09ce9e9-e44a-5f6f-a0ba-e4f831714695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375629} {:answer/id #uuid "394ebbf2-c2d3-501a-9d24-4f85814641c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375630}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375628} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5fabfe3-808f-535a-8196-05aa4185848e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8b4d7dbc-be4c-5d12-8115-af652723cf38" :question/math-academy-id "q-102677" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in \\left(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$ by ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} & =\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\right)}^{2}+1 & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ {\\operatorname{tan}}^{2}t+1 & ={\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf824d4-fe5b-5e61-a294-c20d75a16ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(0,-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375773} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc0d7df-1601-5d99-89b2-1bbbd7a32a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{5}{2},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375775} {:answer/id #uuid "e025c664-543f-5864-b5c3-d3c556a8b3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(0,\\frac{1}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375780} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c51382-9749-5d01-8bb2-48764e6fcb3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{1}{4},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e8179dc-3fed-5f3f-84c9-45a6ccd7804d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375820} {:answer/id #uuid "82d73cb8-e47a-5d28-8a64-a42adeb8a3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375821} {:answer/id #uuid "1d066b56-205b-5a8f-9270-1ad80916efd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375822} {:answer/id #uuid "e12b6e4b-b8e4-5b16-a705-fe2799706827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375823} {:answer/id #uuid "d8da77bd-b62e-5bdc-8ff1-b5a7bca83d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375824} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ea1ff18-caf6-5b75-8df3-01bef44ceaad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c6c00986-d23a-55ec-b3c3-675d7475901f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125123" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of ${\\left(x-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\left(x-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{2}{\\left(x-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${\\left(x-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-4{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)=-4{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-4{\\pi }^{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=2{\\pi }^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${\\left(x-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{2} & =\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{2}={\\pi }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c1e0ab-2828-5e9c-9df8-5e2c181ea0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,(s-u)\\sqrt{3},\\,(s+u)\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375827} {:answer/id #uuid "9e76d983-055b-554b-bc9b-36fe5b1ed5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle -\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375828} {:answer/id #uuid "c3bd6cff-b279-5c03-a2c3-437153fecca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle -\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375829} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e799c3-9581-586b-8d38-bfd28a7079b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375830} {:answer/id #uuid "267277fb-aa68-5bfb-abab-4bd34d2544b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,(s+u)\\sqrt{3},\\,(s-u)\\sqrt{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375828} :answer-field/id #uuid "187786e6-deb0-5d64-8f2a-517c86ffee30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0f4a870e-ec48-5855-9ff2-1d02c3e15a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-127278" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0,$ given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,T(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,-1,1⟩,\\,N(0)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point $P$, which is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The vectors $T(0)$ and $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(0,1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+s\\,T(0)+u\\,N(0) \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨-1,-1,1⟩+u\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "9e76d983-055b-554b-bc9b-36fe5b1ed5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle -\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375828} {:answer/id #uuid "c3bd6cff-b279-5c03-a2c3-437153fecca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle -\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375829} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e799c3-9581-586b-8d38-bfd28a7079b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 1+\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,1-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{s\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{u\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10499cfa-61df-5943-b95e-1ba5310174c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 3,\\,\\frac{4s}{5}-4\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3u}{5}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375834} {:answer/id #uuid "e27f31df-7f2f-59ba-a172-ea8f939d600e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 3,\\,\\frac{4u}{5}-2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{s}{5}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375835} {:answer/id #uuid "35f03cc0-864f-5d7d-b0b0-5a885afe6281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle s+3,\\,4\\pi -\\frac{4u}{5},\\,\\frac{s}{5}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375836} {:answer/id #uuid "fe28e1c6-f74a-5439-a8ef-cf17eb4a9472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle u,\\,4\\pi +\\frac{u}{5},\\,-\\frac{3s}{5}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375837} {:answer/id #uuid "b09d86dd-ee4b-5adc-b5d0-217ed6b01499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 3-u,\\,4\\pi +\\frac{4s}{5},\\,-\\frac{3s}{5}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375838}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375838} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dbd6c2a-0f28-576c-ba0c-678d06d53a28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9466dc08-23c8-53ca-a700-338d9662c848" :question/math-academy-id "q-127282" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\pi ,$ given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,T(\\pi )=\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩,\\,N(\\pi )=⟨-1,0,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point $P$, which is at the tip of the vector $r(\\pi ).$ The vectors $T(\\pi )$ and $N(\\pi )$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(\\pi )+s\\,T(\\pi )+u\\,N(\\pi ),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,4\\pi ,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨3,4\\pi ,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(3,4\\pi ,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(\\pi )+s\\,T(\\pi )+u\\,N(\\pi ) \\\\  & =⟨3,4\\pi ,0⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩+u⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 3-u,\\,4\\pi +\\frac{4s}{5},\\,-\\frac{3s}{5}\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "10499cfa-61df-5943-b95e-1ba5310174c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 3,\\,\\frac{4s}{5}-4\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3u}{5}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375834} {:answer/id #uuid "e27f31df-7f2f-59ba-a172-ea8f939d600e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 3,\\,\\frac{4u}{5}-2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{s}{5}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375835} {:answer/id #uuid "35f03cc0-864f-5d7d-b0b0-5a885afe6281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle s+3,\\,4\\pi -\\frac{4u}{5},\\,\\frac{s}{5}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375836} {:answer/id #uuid "fe28e1c6-f74a-5439-a8ef-cf17eb4a9472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle u,\\,4\\pi +\\frac{u}{5},\\,-\\frac{3s}{5}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375837} {:answer/id #uuid "b09d86dd-ee4b-5adc-b5d0-217ed6b01499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 3-u,\\,4\\pi +\\frac{4s}{5},\\,-\\frac{3s}{5}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275da614-6d1c-5b8f-a6fc-8dbed418f9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨2,\\,1-u,\\,s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375841} {:answer/id #uuid "559b7284-3569-5b64-8108-fcc11b53d182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1,\\,1-2u,\\,s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375842} {:answer/id #uuid "8c56aac5-8c54-5f60-8bab-1f7bebbb106d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨2+s,\\,1,\\,u-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375843} {:answer/id #uuid "80e7c4a0-6258-5587-a7dd-4aecbf57ce65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨2-u,\\,1,\\,1-s⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375844} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd145e7-8104-5746-9dec-ba3461cdc69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨u-2,\\,s-1,\\,u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375845}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375841} :answer-field/id #uuid "19eadaab-818b-52b8-b6c2-db1c5e67db56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b10f792e-96ca-5103-a14b-bd772751151f" :question/math-academy-id "q-127283" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨0,0,1⟩,\\,N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point $P$, which is at the tip of the vector $r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$ The vectors $T(\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and $N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+s\\,T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+u\\,N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\left\\langle 2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right\\rangle =⟨2,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(2,1,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+s\\,T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+u\\,N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =⟨2,1,0⟩+s⟨0,0,1⟩+u⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,1-u,\\,s⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1cb0c5a-9862-57eb-b57f-acb23890d677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375869} {:answer/id #uuid "62e36acb-5f50-5c64-b175-8079a61023a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375870} {:answer/id #uuid "f6dbbdb6-befc-5ff1-81d4-cba020f3cb12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375871} {:answer/id #uuid "13addc19-500d-5e0f-acbb-8b63b95f8faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375872} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2fae7f-e95c-5979-8992-c80d112c68aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375873} :answer-field/id #uuid "6215ee61-981f-5e63-acd6-8f36d8ca0439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c1f2ddde-40be-5a46-987b-92cfeb3c2c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-127321" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-1⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left\\langle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-1\\right\\rangle =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\left\\langle -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},0\\right\\rangle =⟨-1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\left\\langle 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right\\rangle =\\left\\langle 2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ and the principal normal $N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\times N\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,-1,-1⟩\\times ⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|\\left\\langle 2,0,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right\\rangle \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,1,-1⟩| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|0+0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6da15c27-0ab0-56cd-8ef3-27f8c1c55a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375925} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd2612d-5711-599a-a694-c2afac45f762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375926} {:answer/id #uuid "c63764c7-24ed-559f-b365-20b4780612bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375927} {:answer/id #uuid "a55dd6d8-17f7-5ead-b3ba-94f6823a5462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375928} {:answer/id #uuid "35aeb36f-92f3-56c9-80b5-a43746fc2a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375929}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375925} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d564c3b-bc44-527d-b38b-8c6df86c4124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fcaec574-ebde-5bb8-97c7-77da61871c71" :question/math-academy-id "q-131228" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{e}^{t}},\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{e}^{t}}\\right\\rangle ,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{e}^{t}}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{e}^{t}}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}{{e}^{2t}}+\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t}{{e}^{2t}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{e}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{e}^{t}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3a2b495-5ade-5c9a-9d43-e728c9e4e7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375932} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b5f8c0-9dbf-52e2-b352-6bda2e491150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375933} {:answer/id #uuid "8e89e463-6ffe-5364-93ab-503d653f6f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375934} {:answer/id #uuid "7497e593-0737-5859-a271-48d7055fc136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375935} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b1a993-2545-55a0-85bf-22a1c6300847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375936}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375935} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb321e95-8036-5305-85cc-8daac472f444" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19f64431-6e90-56df-bce1-3e6c04731f56" :question/math-academy-id "q-131930" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩,\\,0\\le s<2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\sin }^{2}⁡s+{\\cos }^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c029dea-a78b-581a-8bd5-44c28d695ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375939} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc03ce3-dcb4-5138-85e0-9ac90f62e937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375940} {:answer/id #uuid "2d586d80-2104-5609-95be-63300d4f541c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375941} {:answer/id #uuid "89c05da2-ed4c-5795-b2e7-630412b0a573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375942} {:answer/id #uuid "0da20f0e-5794-567a-8a0a-852e4acd4097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375943} :answer-field/id #uuid "99286ab1-0529-592a-a01e-9213d9a85af3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d12637e4-2147-5039-996f-014616f6dea8" :question/math-academy-id "q-131939" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨s,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩,\\,0\\le s<2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡s+{\\sin }^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4b7733a-d12d-5486-9954-151fe94f6cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376493} {:answer/id #uuid "c04a3865-ba20-52eb-9f69-439e058f6f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376494} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa43bf2-3c36-5fc6-9303-d0653fb81171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376495} {:answer/id #uuid "d008b65a-197f-5787-85ee-62d98a7f8cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376496} :answer-field/id #uuid "9754a5c9-22e3-53ff-b7a1-de2028f9f8cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376492} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0a28c75-6208-5635-879b-1a2f386e11c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376498} {:answer/id #uuid "a20ccace-afeb-5087-ac6d-7499d83888bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376499} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a629e5-abff-5248-af3c-a9040c9b9210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376500} {:answer/id #uuid "f51a1d7e-34c9-5496-b53a-1f0fedc5d984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376501} :answer-field/id #uuid "04fc6168-ea0c-5802-b143-000f81a94492" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376497} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e53ca4-78d6-5d0f-b089-d24c678f99fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ℵ}_{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376503} {:answer/id #uuid "e64b9df8-8815-5a45-b1be-d5681b14d495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376504} {:answer/id #uuid "654d0963-d9a3-59f7-8e22-2a6a04361129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376505} {:answer/id #uuid "9d94a57d-1d08-5550-b5de-9ef8858c93fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{ℵ}_{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376505} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2ce0446-97cb-50be-9325-3fdbedcbb0fa" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e59adc9d-1486-5639-aee0-0f6f33d30bc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-183625" :question/problem "Given that $|R\\times R|=c,$ complete the following reasoning about the cardinality of the set $R\\times [-5,6):$\n\nSince the function $f:(x,y)\\to (x,y)$ (that maps any ordered pair to itself) is an injection from $R\\times [-5,6)$ into $R\\times R,$ and \n\n$$\ng:(x,y)\\to (x,{{field-1}}{{field-2}})\n$$\n\nis an injection from $R\\times R$ onto $R\\times (-5,5)⊆R\\times [-5,6),$ according to Cantor-Bernstein-Schröder theorem, we have that \n$|R\\times [-5,6)|=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "According to the Cantor-Bernstein-Schröder theorem, if $|A|\\le |B|$ and $|B|\\le |A|,$ then $|A|=|B|.$ \n\nIn other words, if there exist injections $f:A\\to B$ and $g:B\\to A,$ then there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The function $f:(x,y)\\to (x,y)$ (that maps any ordered pair to itself) is an injection from $R\\times [-5,6)$ into $R\\times R.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n|R\\times [-5,6)|\\le |R\\times R|.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the function\n\n$$\nx\\to x\n$$\n\ninjectively maps $R\\to R,$ and \n\n$$\ny\\to \\frac{10}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y)\n$$\n\ninjectively maps $R$ onto $(-5,5)⊆[-5,6).$ Thus,\n\n$$\ng:(x,y)\\to \\left(x,\\frac{10}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(y)\\right)\n$$\n\nis an injection from $R\\times R$ onto $R\\times (-5,5)⊆R\\times [-5,6),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/af/af4472ba9056eaf9c62c7dca99de4904339aff6dbf0de8d1225bc41b0fde9c00.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n|R\\times R|\\le |R\\times [-5,6)|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Cantor-Bernstein-Schröder theorem, \n\n$$\n|R\\times [-5,6)|=|R\\times R|=c.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114a6959-d574-5de7-8989-f4eee535ee32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376554} {:answer/id #uuid "a575f88a-da0e-5c56-9a73-33bed9e944de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "=" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376555} {:answer/id #uuid "3e128bb8-c726-5094-9b24-3e7a708d7e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376556}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376556} :answer-field/id #uuid "3498dfaa-de46-5c8a-b515-f3e953cd5eae" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376553} {:answer-field/choice