ere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-2{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=-2$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}\\left[f\\left(-2+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}\\right] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}\\left[f\\left(-2+\\frac{5k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}\\right] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}\\left[-2{\\left(-2+\\frac{5k}{n}\\right)}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "516433a3-ddcf-5286-911f-d1661221641b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}\\left[-2{\\left(\\frac{5k}{n}\\right)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393266} {:answer/id #uuid "91a60e1f-ff52-5d12-8845-6de6b6f23736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}\\left[-2{\\left(-2+\\frac{5k}{n}\\right)}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393267} {:answer/id #uuid "6006a006-5f33-5ef4-b609-573993a53f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}\\left[-2{\\left(2+\\frac{k}{n}\\right)}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393268} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff05667-12f6-5f44-9206-bc6cf51be751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}\\left[-{\\left(-1+\\frac{5k}{n}\\right)}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393269} {:answer/id #uuid "af9dfa31-4950-5539-b718-f5c060dd0d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}\\left[-2\\left(5+\\frac{2k}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ed10303-abcb-5fbd-b21a-2926d9f054e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(2+\\frac{4j}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393273} {:answer/id #uuid "1488f4e3-74cc-56ab-a421-5e3963b43562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393274} {:answer/id #uuid "843176f0-4241-552c-83d3-361908acf549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[2\\sqrt{6+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393275} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa1ac2f-9932-5ea5-85a6-30bb5bb1fe33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(6+\\frac{4j}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393276} {:answer/id #uuid "217ee124-d8cd-5de9-a836-21a2d6d072fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(2+\\frac{4j}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393273} :answer-field/id #uuid "e168e9f1-0828-5482-b9ff-c3095fc465cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d76772d8-d5b1-522d-b84a-c52ac6da6060" :question/math-academy-id "q-73182" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=2$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-2}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[f\\left(2+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[f\\left(2+\\frac{4j}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(2+\\frac{4j}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed10303-abcb-5fbd-b21a-2926d9f054e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(2+\\frac{4j}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393273} {:answer/id #uuid "1488f4e3-74cc-56ab-a421-5e3963b43562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393274} {:answer/id #uuid "843176f0-4241-552c-83d3-361908acf549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[2\\sqrt{6+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393275} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa1ac2f-9932-5ea5-85a6-30bb5bb1fe33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(6+\\frac{4j}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393276} {:answer/id #uuid "217ee124-d8cd-5de9-a836-21a2d6d072fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\left[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(2+\\frac{4j}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92574954-56db-5e22-8577-4044312d87ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $\\left(0,0,\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393287} {:answer/id #uuid "d26ac35c-3433-55c1-aba9-04ac4fb4be37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(0,0,\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(0,0,\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393288} {:answer/id #uuid "da7d850c-c7da-5536-9a3e-8dfd4dedca68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393289} {:answer/id #uuid "9f21d78c-dfb5-5f80-8f9d-1894eb115cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(0,0,\\frac{9}{4}\\right)$ and $\\left(0,0,\\frac{7}{4}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393290} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2f6bf3-3c38-5c3a-982b-11d71b8fcc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(0,0,\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393288} :answer-field/id #uuid "4865ffc5-356e-5dc2-8e39-a104fadf276d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "968294df-641c-514f-b1fc-74e1ba659362" :question/math-academy-id "q-73215" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2}=1,\n$$ find its intercepts with the $z$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{8}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ (z-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ z-2 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =2\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $\\left(0,0,\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(0,0,\\frac{5}{2}\\right).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "92574954-56db-5e22-8577-4044312d87ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $\\left(0,0,\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393287} {:answer/id #uuid "d26ac35c-3433-55c1-aba9-04ac4fb4be37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(0,0,\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(0,0,\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393288} {:answer/id #uuid "9f21d78c-dfb5-5f80-8f9d-1894eb115cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(0,0,\\frac{9}{4}\\right)$ and $\\left(0,0,\\frac{7}{4}\\right)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393290} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2f6bf3-3c38-5c3a-982b-11d71b8fcc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\left(0,0,\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50092f99-331e-5da9-baa1-63a75e661122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2+{t}^{2}}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393454} {:answer/id #uuid "faaf4057-27f2-52cb-8969-f405975a2082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393455} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ba93ae-23c1-56b8-ae52-d7d98f7876b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393456} {:answer/id #uuid "1f529733-dc55-5e3e-8f13-936046ba0713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4+{t}^{2}}}⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393457} {:answer/id #uuid "2a914905-9562-5f7c-8a47-5ab457bde6a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4+{t}^{2}}}⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393458}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393458} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2013858-b8b4-573e-b687-0781e89671d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1aa2119a-8b60-5182-b6db-470b5d45c888" :question/math-academy-id "q-74894" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ at an arbitrary point where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),\\,y(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{t}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)+{t}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4+{t}^{2}}}⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ec9aa97-f0f7-5a10-8249-361b901e466a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393746} {:answer/id #uuid "de4add72-0cd4-537b-80b7-0002434bbdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1-\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393747} {:answer/id #uuid "250743fd-4960-51b1-885b-3ed93deac3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=⟨1+3s,\\,2s+3u,\\,2s-3u⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393748} {:answer/id #uuid "07171637-65de-5c83-8235-9dc05a85624e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393749} {:answer/id #uuid "28f201ce-0d59-5c71-83a0-18e65a037a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 2+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,3+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393749} :answer-field/id #uuid "78aa28f8-2396-55a6-bbc4-e9955564a261" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf74894d-e339-586f-8261-369741108e3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-77651" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the osculating plane for the curve  $r(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, given that\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,T(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\,N(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The osculating plane passes through the point that is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$ The unit tangent vector $T(0)$ and the principal normal vector $N(0)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, a parametric equation for the plane can be written as \n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(0)+sT(0)+uN(0),\\,s,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, a parametric equation for the osculating plane at the point $(1,\\,0,\\,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(0)+sT(0)+uN(0) \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩+s\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec9aa97-f0f7-5a10-8249-361b901e466a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393746} {:answer/id #uuid "de4add72-0cd4-537b-80b7-0002434bbdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1-\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393747} {:answer/id #uuid "07171637-65de-5c83-8235-9dc05a85624e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,1+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393749} {:answer/id #uuid "28f201ce-0d59-5c71-83a0-18e65a037a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left\\langle 2+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}},\\,1-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,3+\\frac{s}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "734a6a1e-e685-50e7-b883-a6ebf4635869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left(-1+\\frac{3}{5}s\\right)\\,i-u\\,j+\\left(1-\\frac{4}{5}s\\right)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393788} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab3dae2-3103-59c9-b9f5-5cbd28d86cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=(5+3s)\\,i-5(u-1)\\,j+(5-4s)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393789} {:answer/id #uuid "f7703ddf-fee7-58ad-92ad-45013706727d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left(\\frac{3}{5}-s\\right)\\,i-u\\,j+\\left(\\frac{4}{5}+s\\right)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393790} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9dae4c-d726-549b-acce-d5842fd2d48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left(1+\\frac{3}{5}s\\right)\\,i-(1+u)\\,j+\\left(1-\\frac{4}{5}s\\right)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393791} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae919b6-296b-5d05-a059-8fab72f07ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=(-1+3s)\\,i-\\,j+(1-4s)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393788} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aefb939-f80c-517f-8459-04195e8fbb29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0afd69e0-1da5-5a6a-895c-0f0f5cbf2711" :question/math-academy-id "q-78206" :question/problem "Find the equation of the osculating plane (in parametric form) for a curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(-1,0,1)$ where $t=\\pi $, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\pi )=\\frac{3}{5}\\,i-\\frac{4}{5}k,\\,N(\\pi )=-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of the point $P(-1,0,1)$ is $r(\\pi )=-i+\\,k.$\n\nThe osculating plane passes through the point that is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$ The vectors $T(1)$ and $N(1)$ are parallel to this plane. Therefore, the equation of the plane (in parametric form) can be written as\n\n$$\nR(s,u)=r(\\pi )+s\\,T(\\pi )+u\\,N(\\pi ),\\,s,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the equation of the osculating plane at the point $(-1,0,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s,u) & =r(\\pi )+s\\,T(\\pi )+u\\,N(\\pi ) \\\\  & =(-i+\\,k)+s\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\,i-\\frac{4}{5}k\\right)+u(-\\,j) \\\\  & =\\left(-1+\\frac{3}{5}s\\right)\\,i-u\\,j+\\left(1-\\frac{4}{5}s\\right)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "734a6a1e-e685-50e7-b883-a6ebf4635869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left(-1+\\frac{3}{5}s\\right)\\,i-u\\,j+\\left(1-\\frac{4}{5}s\\right)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393788} {:answer/id #uuid "f7703ddf-fee7-58ad-92ad-45013706727d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left(\\frac{3}{5}-s\\right)\\,i-u\\,j+\\left(\\frac{4}{5}+s\\right)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393790} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9dae4c-d726-549b-acce-d5842fd2d48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s,u)=\\left(1+\\frac{3}{5}s\\right)\\,i-(1+u)\\,j+\\left(1-\\frac{4}{5}s\\right)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f69ec878-4b9a-5539-8725-c4b325a49f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j-\\frac{2}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393837} {:answer/id #uuid "29d6e8f7-1219-53bf-a56c-456c94e0e5b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j-\\frac{1}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393838} {:answer/id #uuid "3db98340-dc8e-5a06-8c2d-d1c9576c6c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393839} {:answer/id #uuid "24de4534-9c4a-5a84-9729-1290e51ab1ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{25}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393840} {:answer/id #uuid "8be4381f-753d-5f66-b1b1-28c473efbdb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j+\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393839} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d079c9-27cf-5f55-a153-92a8cb90ae4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "72f93e03-a33f-50d8-b52b-f8964fad8ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-78349" :question/problem "Given the curve  $r(t)=t\\,i+\\sqrt{8}t\\,j-4t\\,k,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=5t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=5t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{5}.$  \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{5}\\,i+\\left(\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\frac{s}{5}\\right)j-4\\cdot \\frac{s}{5}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}s}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4s}{5}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{s}{5}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{8}s}{5}\\right)j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{4s}{5}\\right)k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f744694c-a7e5-531b-b1a0-b70a89880b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{9}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393851} {:answer/id #uuid "90720291-67d0-5e1a-b546-8a1aea34b932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393852} {:answer/id #uuid "8fcf0b34-e534-51fc-97fb-64d7d6fafa02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393853} {:answer/id #uuid "07fc2226-9b81-5440-8648-0026a95bbbe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{18},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393854} {:answer/id #uuid "edbdf1ca-35ec-595a-a27c-e608c973c72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{81},\\,\\frac{4}{81},\\,\\frac{8}{81}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393851} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fefcfb2-453a-5854-be55-e93605da6418" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "56897adb-706a-5c81-8930-6f2aa15c0be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-78404" :question/problem "Given the curve  $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{2},\\,8{t}^{2}⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=9{t}^{2},$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=9{t}^{2}$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{s}{9} \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left\\langle {\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3}\\right)}^{2},\\,4{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3}\\right)}^{2},\\,8{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3}\\right)}^{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{s}{9},\\,\\frac{4s}{9},\\,\\frac{8s}{9}\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{s}{9}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{4s}{9}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{8s}{9}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{9}\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "f744694c-a7e5-531b-b1a0-b70a89880b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{9}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393851} {:answer/id #uuid "90720291-67d0-5e1a-b546-8a1aea34b932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393852} {:answer/id #uuid "8fcf0b34-e534-51fc-97fb-64d7d6fafa02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393853} {:answer/id #uuid "07fc2226-9b81-5440-8648-0026a95bbbe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{18},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393854} {:answer/id #uuid "edbdf1ca-35ec-595a-a27c-e608c973c72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{81},\\,\\frac{4}{81},\\,\\frac{8}{81}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea4d47ac-28b7-5cf4-9951-7b77a964f98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393949} {:answer/id #uuid "cf86acff-f5b4-55bc-b223-41ec5c107c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{t}\\right){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393950} {:answer/id #uuid "ae341b9f-f082-5951-b90d-651bde7b870e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-t\\right){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393951} {:answer/id #uuid "1760a04f-1eaa-5c8a-94d3-d99f8b0366d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=({t}^{2}+2){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393952} {:answer/id #uuid "57bf20e1-cc81-5b9c-8911-369ec514af71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=2{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393949} :answer-field/id #uuid "8de45fec-6d7a-5cbb-8c75-d5637ebdb1b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1bb8ed71-56ab-536b-a7bf-0caa82667de7" :question/math-academy-id "q-79620" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+4t{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{t},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{c}(t)=\\frac{A}{t}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}},\n$$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ to this equation. \n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:* \n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}+C\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(t){y}^{′}+Q(t)y=f(t).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${t}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4}{t}{y}^{′}+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}y=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{t}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=\\frac{1}{t},\\,{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{′}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{′}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\left(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{4}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =-\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{2}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}, \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{f(t){y}_{1}(t)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}t=-\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(1-t){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(t) & ={y}_{1}(t)u(t)+{y}_{2}(t)v(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot (1-t){e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}{e}^{t}+\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}\\right){e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{t}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}\\right){e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "cf86acff-f5b4-55bc-b223-41ec5c107c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{t}\\right){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393950} {:answer/id #uuid "ae341b9f-f082-5951-b90d-651bde7b870e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(t)=\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-t\\right){e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eae9680e-fb0b-5d5c-a43b-045035e1a6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393977} {:answer/id #uuid "099a69ab-5d15-5dea-9c8e-a1798d5df706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393978} {:answer/id #uuid "76998400-989a-502e-87a5-f47b2cc7d5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393979} {:answer/id #uuid "39d0f20d-84d5-5b9a-b19f-d54e9b2367a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393980} {:answer/id #uuid "f225a2ee-489a-5511-a2a3-7053e8c82039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393978} :answer-field/id #uuid "660359e4-8229-50fa-93c3-3698c31ad9df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87924d0a-6b63-5a30-a55c-848153ca642c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79768" :question/problem "The substitution $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=xy\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{z}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{z}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{z}=x{e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{z}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z\\right) & =x{e}^{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f349ccaf-635c-5643-92d8-6ff7fffcacfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨121,15⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394492} {:answer/id #uuid "24cc6a45-71de-50bc-a394-3e20bc5a7ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨32,-49⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394493} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebb1330-51e4-5cb7-af9d-c81f72807962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨85,-8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394494} {:answer/id #uuid "b23e6a9c-17ef-592d-98ac-3399ef06798e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨-66,21⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394495} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8efdf2-2841-5402-b342-6b954fa1a59f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(1)=⟨112,10⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394492} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f50a45-adc6-5b16-876f-809ec791c11f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb1ed2a5-10d4-520a-8be1-03271b56ffd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-83497" :question/problem "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨12(1+2t)^{3},9(1-3t)^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,6⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨12(1+2t)^{3},9(1-3t)^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 12(1+2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 9(1-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+{C}_{2}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨1,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{2}(1+2(0))^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1-3(0))^{3}+{C}_{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ ⟨1,6⟩ & =\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{2}+{C}_{1},\\,-1+{C}_{2}\\right\\rangle ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${C}_{2}=7.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{2}(1+2t)^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3t)^{3}+7\\right\\rangle .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{2}(1+2(1))^{4}-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-(1-3(1))^{3}+7\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨121,15⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c77ea9bc-2c0d-561d-a364-eb7c29c4f841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394506} {:answer/id #uuid "ddadced8-2143-5670-94ef-c5d98f93192e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sec}\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394507} {:answer/id #uuid "8dff8e1c-ebb3-52f2-8a15-a57dc0d7bbff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394508} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5f2fd4-bdfe-5a77-bfc6-f8822f475f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394509} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9e64dc-cb50-57f4-a11f-0e91717eba0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394510}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394510} :answer-field/id #uuid "70a6a823-aaff-53a1-8971-3dd47ec005cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f578d29d-0823-5f21-864f-baa92f133476" :question/math-academy-id "q-83501" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\,y\\le 0\\},\n$$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/61/617a69d44661d03e1c170b0c397d11f6437a7222c286df5023154371ec90ab4d.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $\\left(-\\frac{1}{2},0\\right).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {\\left(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/2d/2d50f2375c071d8bfb4c5c58a836bef76a70557c2593d4df9acf48f9f0e7527b.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/98/98e9c8442c17cbc77083c5dc55a9d74324befa05e3cd47e1c65c38cd510c08c6.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8daaa863-4ed5-528c-8dbf-f411602a18b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394547} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff14fdd-eefc-5322-8abf-2722d438a026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394548} {:answer/id #uuid "b2216cb8-dda6-543b-bea0-78a155f39c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394549} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9fdf78-2c9f-596d-b102-140d5e888ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394550} {:answer/id #uuid "49a2ca2d-1ffc-59a5-8706-fbb1eada3fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394547} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0a886ac-bf1c-524b-87ca-5608d2086121" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1b9a1961-cfec-5ea0-aa17-358f76216410" :question/math-academy-id "q-83955" :question/problem "If $$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{2}+{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\hspace{0.222em}x\\le 0\\},\n$$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/32/32c4bf490df51121ddf1575df8211033185d392fd5b08519971723b820da191f.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $\\left(0,\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}+{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{\\left(y-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+{\\left(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/b1/b1e2eac0041d0b0cb802561b3fb83fd8fab1b940cb6c42f698879837c06cbff0.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/19/193601785ff94605d12b91b7cab1e7390af532ba1912530bd8d3f492324e2788.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc4323e-c33f-535a-afd4-6c18fb62876c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{4},-2\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394615} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b5c9f8-a768-5571-978b-75dd487db682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{1}{4},-5\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394617} {:answer/id #uuid "22da317e-ddf5-5200-804d-bd894039dd72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(5,-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eb92dbf-5fad-5efe-b557-19b88bef10da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle \\frac{t}{2},\\,\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394677} {:answer/id #uuid "7923ff02-537a-5c46-9034-26ed6657df17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=⟨2t,\\,{e}^{2t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394678} {:answer/id #uuid "c449ee8e-b0e5-5fff-8c3d-384f4cf10aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle \\frac{t}{2},\\,\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{4}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394679} {:answer/id #uuid "72759446-2ed5-55d7-ad38-e3108b39c53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=⟨2t,\\,2{e}^{2t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394680} {:answer/id #uuid "c55320d0-6670-59d6-8213-9d8ccd909743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=⟨t,\\,{e}^{2t}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394680} :answer-field/id #uuid "a877d503-72cd-5d28-9807-236e281f86ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2ba6af89-af5f-56af-a9e0-160f4f71aca0" :question/math-academy-id "q-85598" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{2t}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,2{e}^{2t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb92dbf-5fad-5efe-b557-19b88bef10da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle \\frac{t}{2},\\,\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394677} {:answer/id #uuid "c449ee8e-b0e5-5fff-8c3d-384f4cf10aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle \\frac{t}{2},\\,\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{4}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab87df10-dd7f-5124-963d-bf4c394614d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle 18,\\,\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394684} {:answer/id #uuid "c63d9c83-e34e-558e-8ae3-fc836736d6c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle 18t,\\,-\\frac{2}{t}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394685} {:answer/id #uuid "38b37a91-571a-5339-b19f-1605a1381473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle 9{t}^{2}+1,\\,\\frac{1}{t}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394686} {:answer/id #uuid "3db78a2c-6537-5296-80b7-167fd4035b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle 18t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394687} {:answer/id #uuid "66b578b5-b668-5062-b023-bcdcd606cfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle 9{t}^{2}+1,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394687} :answer-field/id #uuid "a517e4e9-c7f1-5c48-b08e-bfc1ef17b5e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fd766578-35eb-5e9e-b4c9-c03b5909e8a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-85606" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3{t}^{3}+t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{3}+t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 9{t}^{2}+1,\\,\\frac{1}{t}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(9{t}^{2}+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 18t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "ab87df10-dd7f-5124-963d-bf4c394614d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle 18,\\,\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394684} {:answer/id #uuid "c63d9c83-e34e-558e-8ae3-fc836736d6c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle 18t,\\,-\\frac{2}{t}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394685} {:answer/id #uuid "38b37a91-571a-5339-b19f-1605a1381473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle 9{t}^{2}+1,\\,\\frac{1}{t}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394686} {:answer/id #uuid "3db78a2c-6537-5296-80b7-167fd4035b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle 18t,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394687} {:answer/id #uuid "66b578b5-b668-5062-b023-bcdcd606cfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a=\\left\\langle 9{t}^{2}+1,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3eb1023-8fe9-5f8a-9ed6-e733f9b55470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨-1,24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394691} {:answer/id #uuid "255a3bd0-0ade-5cd9-be87-ddbf8293d7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨0,48⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394692} {:answer/id #uuid "bffa36e6-c392-5dbf-94e3-7f1a95af1a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨1,96⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394693} {:answer/id #uuid "865d7135-3b82-57df-a140-3dd131381f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨0,24⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394694} {:answer/id #uuid "788dada1-a631-5db1-8aea-9bc72816dcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨1,48⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394695} :answer-field/id #uuid "66aac930-2d92-52fb-a347-e888b276edb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c35299e-e7ea-52d7-8411-4ed07a1c0894" :question/math-academy-id "q-85609" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{e}^{2-t},\\,3+{t}^{4}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2-t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+{t}^{4})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{2-t},\\,4{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{2-t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2-t},\\,12{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =⟨{e}^{2-2},12(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,48⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e583a002-70fe-56cb-970c-6ecf4707b90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394938} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4ddc40-d054-5df0-98ae-97135292007a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{6}{x}-x\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\left(3-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394939} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c927ca-6db7-5bda-8854-a4c3997acd27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{6}{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394940} {:answer/id #uuid "23524752-4ff0-543f-875d-cc1e4346d1ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{1}{x}-x\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394941} {:answer/id #uuid "20bfb43c-eb37-5a4d-9164-7d5d054c1f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=(6-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+({x}^{2}-3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394942}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394939} :answer-field/id #uuid "817d71d9-2b7f-5b80-b0c4-ed70b708eece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2524797a-65ac-5664-a2f2-3a154d0cd5f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94191" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-4y={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(x)=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{2}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. \n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:* \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=4x({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+({x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+24)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}({x}^{2})^{′}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}^{′}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2x\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right){x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}(4x({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+({x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+24)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x), \\\\ v(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}(4x({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+({x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+24)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\right)+({x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-x+\\frac{6}{x}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+3-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{6}{x}-x\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\left(3-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4ddc40-d054-5df0-98ae-97135292007a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{6}{x}-x\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\left(3-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394939} {:answer/id #uuid "23524752-4ff0-543f-875d-cc1e4346d1ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{1}{x}-x\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb155aa0-4397-5e09-819c-177cf354209d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394945} {:answer/id #uuid "3a60be13-f954-54e0-8a80-78c79f0e908e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394946} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcea938-3524-5ff6-82a3-95314a4e82b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394947} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2a3fc9-195a-5fea-9901-9cb1d649dade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394948} {:answer/id #uuid "9196c699-6cbf-59be-ad95-ea91912b2959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394947} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f9af7bf-8e4b-52c1-9def-7c956fc990ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a237827c-e559-5ab9-ade9-5d51e7b60d0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94226" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+3y=x{e}^{x},\\,x>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{c}(x)=\\frac{A}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{B}{x},\n$$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. \n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:* \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6){e}^{x}+C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=(x-1){e}^{x}+C\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{′}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}^{′}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\right)\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6){e}^{x}, \\\\ v(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}(x-1){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6){e}^{x}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{2}(x-1){e}^{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2x}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2x}\\right){e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "bb155aa0-4397-5e09-819c-177cf354209d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394945} {:answer/id #uuid "3a60be13-f954-54e0-8a80-78c79f0e908e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394946} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcea938-3524-5ff6-82a3-95314a4e82b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394947} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2a3fc9-195a-5fea-9901-9cb1d649dade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394948} {:answer/id #uuid "9196c699-6cbf-59be-ad95-ea91912b2959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\left(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\right){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e7115a-e920-5ca2-a52d-4d4470e51253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}i+4\\sqrt{2}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395318} {:answer/id #uuid "3bfe3a65-f7eb-5cac-b9df-1d99a81db488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395319} {:answer/id #uuid "ba5b7e1f-c70c-5336-b4a3-8a0a5c072f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{2}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395320} {:answer/id #uuid "64836fb0-ce3b-5203-837b-1b97e9240fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}i-4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395321} {:answer/id #uuid "a6dffde1-0f1f-568f-96fb-f9a4047c7c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4i+4\\sqrt{2}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395322}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395318} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea875676-d650-522d-87a7-9a80af4b93cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba9f4197-bf38-5147-a088-adef74f86d38" :question/math-academy-id "q-97666" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(4,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=4$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}\\,i+4\\sqrt{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "615b3966-ab34-54c4-94fb-2b6a493a05bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395325} {:answer/id #uuid "ba942577-688b-51be-af1b-e90b9481cd96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40\\,i-40\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395326} {:answer/id #uuid "2a94b620-9112-5178-8c25-19f0c2cb69b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-80\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395327} {:answer/id #uuid "32c6ee0a-9437-5374-a41d-a5b0b40cfef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-80\\,i+80\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395328} {:answer/id #uuid "c996e548-9d94-5d06-8643-4a5b9cd9f062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395329}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395327} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30d24cc-d490-51a6-9670-d0e1ff6bece7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b9b0109c-14cb-5899-8302-a8286694f506" :question/math-academy-id "q-97673" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $4\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(5,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=5$ and $\\omega =4\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(5(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+20\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-20\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+20\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-80\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-80\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =-80\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =-80\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98e66298-a08d-5d19-86a9-54be284c9700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,{\\text{ms}}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395332} {:answer/id #uuid "fc48ed15-6ee6-5a09-b4a5-0175bd5cd89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,{\\text{ms}}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395333} {:answer/id #uuid "d1aee831-1742-5b62-bf17-ddc658ee0b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0\\,{\\text{ms}}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395334} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd311ea-9fff-555b-8dbe-5f5a9c9375be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{ms}}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395335} {:answer/id #uuid "fddb5016-b670-52f9-814d-2fb50e87a148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{\\text{ms}}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395334} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b0f922d-a304-5edb-af59-b51332258ba6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "98416008-933f-5c1e-b3dd-bccbe15a9b73" :question/math-academy-id "q-97763" :question/problem "The position $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t⟩,$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the tangential component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=\\pi \\,\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The tangential component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{T}=\\frac{v\\cdot a}{||v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector and evaluating it at $t=\\pi ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,1⟩, \\\\ v(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector and it evaluating at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩, \\\\ a(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $v(\\pi )\\cdot a(\\pi )$ and $||v(\\pi )||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\pi )\\cdot a(\\pi ) & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =0, \\\\ ||v(\\pi )|| & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the tangential component of the acceleration at $t=\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{T} & =\\frac{v(\\pi )\\cdot a(\\pi )}{||v(\\pi )||} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangential component of the acceleration is $0\\,{\\text{ms}}^{-2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f693057-c72f-59fd-bb85-e1d3b3b8f025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-4,\\,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395432} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb4baaa-dad8-5e2b-b479-60014247b94c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395433} {:answer/id #uuid "99943121-a1e7-54c3-8131-a21d92a15c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-7,\\,2,\\,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395434} {:answer/id #uuid "0efc8db7-bfc3-5939-a763-1aecad7fa1b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-7,\\,-4,\\,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395435} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1d135d-9328-52b4-8886-86201387e324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-4,\\,-3,\\,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395436}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395435} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddea707b-3da8-5efc-8f78-57e4551cffb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f54ef51-56ed-57bf-ac96-48cf9f9bc402" :question/math-academy-id "q-98158" :question/problem "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\left[\\frac{1}{2}f(t)-g(t)\\right]\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ \ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-6,\\,-2,\\,8⟩\n$$\nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,3,\\,-1⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\left[\\frac{1}{2}f(t)-g(t)\\right]\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\left[\\frac{1}{2}f(t)-g(t)\\right]\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{0}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-6,\\,-2,\\,8⟩-⟨4,\\,3,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,-1,\\,4⟩-⟨4,\\,3,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,\\,-4,\\,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fa697c9-4d76-5898-8fb5-ac84a77debdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}-3,\\,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395439} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8a89ab-8c73-517f-8e47-53ad9f4c06fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨8+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}+1,\\,-12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395440} {:answer/id #uuid "b9fcf75b-c1d0-5f39-9056-7c5640eb928e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-8+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,1,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395441} {:answer/id #uuid "1423503f-a4b1-5ae9-ba3b-0e1efa315d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395442} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0fdc34-4ee8-516c-b880-42f9c2fcec55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}+1,\\,-8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395443} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5f819ae-49fc-5c26-94b2-5e3cdd0e1a0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4c2ff90f-3df2-5a7a-ae8c-1f49f03b53d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-98170" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}[f(t)+4g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}-1,\\,2⟩\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{5}{2}\\right\\rangle .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the vector addition and scalar multiplication rules, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}[f(t)+4g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+4{\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}[f(t)+4g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+4{\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}-1,\\,2⟩+4\\left\\langle \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{5}{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}-1,\\,2⟩+⟨3,\\,2,\\,-10⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}+1,\\,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0dfad44-fa97-5c53-b9c0-6604bf641ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨8,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395611} {:answer/id #uuid "0661ad18-e453-551e-b6a3-3b9a7fd4cc3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨12,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395612} {:answer/id #uuid "9b12bbeb-f0bb-5166-9997-72fc2e3788db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨12,\\,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395613} {:answer/id #uuid "d821aeda-21d3-5c5d-9e75-86510d821b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨4,\\,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395614} {:answer/id #uuid "f706e041-e41e-54fa-8158-43e0674160c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨20,\\,8⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395612} :answer-field/id #uuid "41526ae9-61f1-5b5f-92ee-a7e06097aa76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6a4c9ec-988e-50b9-a11d-a4ebe9c71ccb" :question/math-academy-id "q-99278" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\left\\langle 2t-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\right\\rangle \\text{d}t=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each componentt separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\left\\langle 2t-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\right\\rangle \\text{d}t & =\\left\\langle {\\int }_{1}^{4}(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t,{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨({t}^{2}-t)\\,\\,{|}_{1}^{4},\\,\\sqrt{t}\\,\\,{|}_{1}^{4}\\,\\,⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\text{ }({4}^{2}-4)-({1}^{2}-1),\\,\\sqrt{4}-\\sqrt{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66b455d9-9dcf-5eb5-8c79-f1c4b2c17a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395639} {:answer/id #uuid "e94dd2e1-84d5-5f65-9fcb-29972b9c2104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2\\sqrt{2},\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395640} {:answer/id #uuid "19fb725c-dfdb-554b-9ee0-5ea47a6fa6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395641} {:answer/id #uuid "4f175d91-95fe-5e94-86f8-2c07fa5e2233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\sqrt{2},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395642} {:answer/id #uuid "ac22ded7-29b9-55aa-a4fc-490f512ab8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395641} :answer-field/id #uuid "734ada2e-fef2-5740-8c5f-7c0d921da24b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ce4de9d0-a35b-5282-8a54-5ba3b82cc5ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-99366" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{\\sec }^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each componentt separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{\\sec }^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\sec }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi /4)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0),\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi /4)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0,\\,1-0\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395644 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e40088-fc75-5f03-afdb-4419e14b0c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨{3}^{t},\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395646} {:answer/id #uuid "84bf4860-2cb7-5bfe-a96b-c459981516d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395647} {:answer/id #uuid "d0978271-3766-55c7-bd73-80b04d362f12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t⟩+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395648} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9d76f1-1a04-5098-a9d1-d93d96357185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2},\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395649} {:answer/id #uuid "9e50a43d-3d69-5f77-b032-f2c078b14d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395647} :answer-field/id #uuid "12919900-7f26-5b8f-ad34-67b95659b570" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "056c0a76-101b-521d-8188-2f2f8cee2913" :question/math-academy-id "q-99392" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ⟨{3}^{t},\\,{\\csc }^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{3}^{t},\\,{\\csc }^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int {3}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {\\csc }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+{c}_{2}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\right\\rangle +⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\right\\rangle +C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "84bf4860-2cb7-5bfe-a96b-c459981516d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\\,-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395647} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9d76f1-1a04-5098-a9d1-d93d96357185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2},\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395649} {:answer/id #uuid "9e50a43d-3d69-5f77-b032-f2c078b14d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle \\frac{{3}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3},\\,-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\right\\rangle +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91004853-1346-560b-a5d3-ff22bfcebfda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-1,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395688} {:answer/id #uuid "116a3ed5-0a5b-5b9f-9605-bf1f43cb3d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+1,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395689} {:answer/id #uuid "12041586-56b6-51e9-9f26-854cd04ef038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-t|,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395690} {:answer/id #uuid "007ec25e-10c4-57e6-8f6a-1b8e25331e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-1,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395691} {:answer/id #uuid "9b87310d-4b2e-5dc6-a9de-c014aec90d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle -\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+2\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395692}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395688} :answer-field/id #uuid "6907501d-a30c-54b1-9db2-2cf30d318d4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e321419c-a366-5737-8cc6-0eb09e45ddb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-99546" :question/problem "If ${F}^{′}(t)=\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{t},\\,\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\right\\rangle $ and $F(1)=⟨-1,\\,2⟩,$ then $F(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\left\\langle \\frac{1}{t},\\,\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\right\\rangle \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left\\langle \\frac{1}{t},\\,\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\right\\rangle \\,\\text{d}t & =\\left\\langle \\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\text{d}t\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(1)=⟨-1,\\,2⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|+{c}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨{c}_{1},\\,1+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨-1,\\,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=1.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-1,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩.\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "9b87310d-4b2e-5dc6-a9de-c014aec90d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\langle -\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+2\\right\\rangle " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "653e27c5-93b9-5b8b-a566-b9feceda02bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395695} {:answer/id #uuid "5794d3a1-89b4-5773-b8c5-d7d54c7628e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨0,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395696} {:answer/id #uuid "4cce8c74-35bc-56c3-8c51-9d331c6a850c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395697} {:answer/id #uuid "567d70fa-56b5-55c2-b2e8-1f9b1ce38269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395698} {:answer/id #uuid "7487841f-9614-5183-b897-83cb0df32a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395698} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff474b43-5d6a-5b7a-a58e-1b2570cb72d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c3acb097-24af-576e-b081-d26bf643475d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99574" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-0,-(-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}),-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c00a40ba-01e3-5ee1-8e78-5ca100de2c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395730} {:answer/id #uuid "b95804dd-01ed-5465-b1ec-454869fcea4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{5x+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395731} {:answer/id #uuid "957bc594-a52d-54c5-8fe2-348b94cdf190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{5x+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395732} {:answer/id #uuid "40a36a09-4403-51a9-8c9b-14cdbfe2aa41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395733} {:answer/id #uuid "2458c64b-d709-5fd5-a6bf-7d12e7bb36e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395734}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395733} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bf9bf65-124a-5233-aedc-81c57617266f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "457b9ae0-36a8-577c-85ad-8057dc66fc9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-12498" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (5x+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=5x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5x+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(5x+1)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left[\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C\\right] & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x+1)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(5x+1)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5x+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(5x+1)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5x+1}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1cd77c1-d94a-50e2-a2d2-310f30af1efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{2}{13},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395757} {:answer/id #uuid "932f6c0b-7bfc-5138-a5fc-88d5849899ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{13}{2},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395758} {:answer/id #uuid "62a3c355-7a46-5e69-a9b4-100e157d8443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395759} {:answer/id #uuid "1acd5f2b-ea97-5c92-bd3f-4c0fa0954dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{13}{2},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395760} {:answer/id #uuid "de725257-e555-5095-bd67-53b03fd19c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{2}{13},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395761}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395757} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcb45a2c-578d-5e71-917a-16d23dca9a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2a1f70ec-1a84-5f86-99bd-fdd4752087f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-14191" :question/problem "Find the point of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}+13x-15y-2=0$ with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+13x-15(0)-2 & =0 \\\\ 13x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $\\left(\\frac{2}{13},0\\right).$"} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cd77c1-d94a-50e2-a2d2-310f30af1efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{2}{13},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395757} {:answer/id #uuid "932f6c0b-7bfc-5138-a5fc-88d5849899ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{13}{2},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395758} {:answer/id #uuid "1acd5f2b-ea97-5c92-bd3f-4c0fa0954dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{13}{2},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395760} {:answer/id #uuid "de725257-e555-5095-bd67-53b03fd19c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(-\\frac{2}{13},0\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1ea5dbf-e0e0-5b87-9716-69e18e732d25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395792} {:answer/id #uuid "967e7ed0-7817-52a2-8722-d7d6b2028465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395793} {:answer/id #uuid "ffad3220-17fd-5911-9971-a3e690e83416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395794} {:answer/id #uuid "b99d6896-ed6a-55b6-8e15-065a39717d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395795} {:answer/id #uuid "350623d5-cf3b-562c-adbc-4f53ac07cc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395793} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b9d34d-27a9-54ed-b870-554479215d16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "236c5a80-1a72-598b-b891-11878898b571" :question/math-academy-id "q-2549" :question/problem "![](images/27/2771f90d4200f292afe8108a26b8a2e83e6f3a46194c89a18d1688943984b5af.png)\n\nA spherical balloon is inflated so that its radius increases at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ How fast is the volume of the balloon increasing at the moment when the radius is $2\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,\\text{cm/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=2\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $r=2,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\cdot 4\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =16\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the balloon is increasing at a rate of $16\\pi {\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}$\n when the radius is $2\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "95acc209-e214-5661-af27-7731166005f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7ecb47-348a-575c-b016-4544f1974784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395933} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e2897f-2d72-56a4-bcc5-a06d675b471f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{15}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395934} {:answer/id #uuid "a134fc89-5f1c-5a05-9bfd-f3a9d2290923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395935} {:answer/id #uuid "89511689-d194-586c-b3c5-3518510b8c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395936} {:answer/id #uuid "d372b5ec-aee8-50bb-8607-45719a03988b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{10}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395936} :answer-field/id #uuid "fead9c5f-966c-5594-b2e5-c190cdcde2f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4cc9f66e-fd87-5f99-8445-a69528565ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-7210" :question/problem "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\sqrt{t}-{t}^{3/2}$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{4}{15}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t}-{t}^{3/2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{4}{15}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{4}{15}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}{t}^{3/2}\\left(\\frac{5}{3}-t\\right) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0$, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{s}.$"} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6983c6-2a3a-5a74-9155-3140c3b16817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(5,\\pm \\frac{3}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395946} {:answer/id #uuid "065f5013-a6a4-59df-9c2e-0ed50e4f3f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(5,\\pm \\frac{9}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395948} {:answer/id #uuid "c143c2b9-589b-5044-8878-4be12b83a9e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(5,\\pm \\frac{3}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395949} {:answer/id #uuid "6792e8d3-e7b8-5579-b7f7-7456877be5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(5,\\pm \\frac{9}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbcf88a3-1e20-5a4c-bb86-1fe1bdaa1963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396054} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0d6ea0-c5ed-5e7f-9687-ab98aef0c28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396055} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dc7a4e-aacb-5122-89ac-d5eb5d04a7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$9$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396056} {:answer/id #uuid "950ec668-d2b7-5d89-8e4f-1d349b1209a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396057} {:answer/id #uuid "2789771c-579f-5375-8680-854defcdf4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396055} :answer-field/id #uuid "1984d219-413d-5333-ac39-1acd7d9d0ec5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1270303e-0109-58be-ac65-6aecbfb8540f" :question/math-academy-id "q-14050" :question/problem "![](images/04/0470661491c0d9d44ecbbc3c87af70d4ff334f1ffd2ca56b68bd4c126f3d88de.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,-4)$ and passes through the point $\\left(0,-\\frac{7}{4}\\right).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $\\left(0,-\\frac{7}{4}\\right),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{4}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-3)^{2} & =4p\\left(-\\frac{7}{4}+4\\right) \\\\ 9 & =4p\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(0+4) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $7$ and $-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2fe06dc-0609-5053-9367-73d7ebf2fcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $(-2,-2)$, radius: $\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396068} {:answer/id #uuid "3f353887-7ced-5107-882a-0276e72dd462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $(-2,2)$, radius: $\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396069} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c03ddb-5aef-510c-adf3-fa2a8970e245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $(2,2)$, radius: $\\frac{13}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396070} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5518f9-14b3-5d56-ae49-14acc285d236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $(2,4)$, radius: $\\frac{13}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396071} {:answer/id #uuid "0ddf99ec-bda7-505b-b4fe-67ffc75b34aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "center: $(2,-2)$, radius: $\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396072} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd2771d-4f88-578f-9fd3-675729a93312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0485f61e-5d32-5461-91b2-4119de37bba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-14619" :question/problem "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n$2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-8x+8y+3=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-8x+8y+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x+4y & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x)+({y}^{2}+4y) & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}) & =-\\frac{3}{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+4)+({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+4) & =\\frac{13}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{(y-(-2))}^{2} & ={\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{2}}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(2,-2)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{2}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cc89b8-44ed-5197-913c-4b08fc997159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{6x}$ and $b=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396622} {:answer/id #uuid "e2308dd8-f04b-55a3-a357-4d4562b92a56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396623} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ab5d51-4c5d-5e5c-bd82-b3125ac54774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{6x}$ and $b=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396624} {:answer/id #uuid "68909286-13b5-56f8-91c1-b39ee0c858f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $b=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396625} {:answer/id #uuid "e106c9d7-5e46-53e8-bd3f-34d8e8d97090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396626}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396623} :answer-field/id #uuid "224da74b-2945-552e-a5c2-10b9f76d854e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c2081a2b-f662-517d-bd66-e0ddf60008b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-113918" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}\\left[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}\\right].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{6}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=\\frac{6i}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})={e}^{{x}_{i}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}\\left[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}\\right].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70a234cb-554b-545b-99a9-38ca1d4a7d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396656} {:answer/id #uuid "813b9b1c-2e23-599a-bbed-341899317794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f,g,$ and $h$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396657} {:answer/id #uuid "43827749-fd32-5ade-b947-0ae8c476e27e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396658} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4fa032-2148-54c2-9c32-cbe3a90719b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396659} {:answer/id #uuid "53e9c955-42a6-5020-a355-eae4fe66d4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396660}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396657} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aedcafb-9859-554f-b742-1fe94afe482b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4689ffaf-d7a6-5ea0-9161-3ec67ff19338" :question/math-academy-id "q-341642" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x+1},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd},\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=u(x-1)+u\\,\\left(x-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)+u(x-4).\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/d5/d5a497337426dad07096702a3e211f525a959add8cb1fbaaef05fccac26d634b.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ and $x=4,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 5/{2}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to 5/{2}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e499c456-6a1c-5152-933c-28134d048ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396682} {:answer/id #uuid "f947c798-bc89-571a-a666-d5ac5fe1b843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396683} {:answer/id #uuid "085d6ab0-e2f9-55ab-a353-c2e9ceb0a9dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396684} {:answer/id #uuid "6da93e3c-bfd8-566a-bf85-bf589185d302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396685} {:answer/id #uuid "b72fbfe6-34a0-5341-a7d6-2590a001b91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $b=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396682} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb2a4470-ddb9-5a4f-aab9-d2401a7ffff8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6a62fd18-f127-592a-8fe3-1cc5a47bb7fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-49234" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}\\right].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=\\frac{k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=2{e}^{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}\\right].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6333f256-57a4-524f-882c-85ce13ed4d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397212} {:answer/id #uuid "bdff980e-244b-5407-8c35-36869326e1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397213} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9fce80-afaa-5cf2-a342-0dd3523e12a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397214} {:answer/id #uuid "bc315ebe-9716-5917-b194-2a4a36b373d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{2x}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397215} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce119a8-474d-516f-b714-f6645bb4943e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}x}$ and $Cx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397213} :answer-field/id #uuid "6451528f-6193-5fce-847d-24fbb635dd58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f942b92-7058-551e-856d-94bbeff34573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397218} {:answer/id #uuid "e417753c-3dcf-5b7e-8c3e-55d245a9b3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397219} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff25083-915f-5352-b85b-65678f3833df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397220} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eb5523-b928-553e-8031-34e6f2d343f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{3}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397221} {:answer/id #uuid "182b3678-ac7b-5812-986d-8764905ed502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{3}{14}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397222} :answer-field/id #uuid "81e0c9b5-cbc2-598f-97c0-6c5a9eeadf86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba070694-f520-5989-9c36-fcafe8653bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338837" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-14\\lambda {y}^{′}+49{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=0$ and $y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-14\\lambda m+49{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-7\\lambda )(m-7\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=7\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{7\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{7\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA+B\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=B{e}^{7\\lambda x}+7B\\lambda x{e}^{7\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2B{e}^{14\\lambda }+B{e}^{14\\lambda }+14B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 3B{e}^{14\\lambda }+14B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{14\\lambda }+\\frac{14}{3}B\\lambda {e}^{14\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{14\\lambda }\\left(1+\\frac{14}{3}\\lambda \\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require $B\\ne 0.$ Thus, since ${e}^{14\\lambda }>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{14}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding eigenfunction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =Bx{e}^{7\\lambda x} \\\\  & =Bx{e}^{-\\frac{3}{2}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c73a7b2-a2bf-518a-beb1-31fbbadf0c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6-{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397298} {:answer/id #uuid "d70f278f-f71b-563b-90a5-a72e4490f97d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397299} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9aa96a-e438-54ed-91f2-a9cd9b89c2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{6{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397300} {:answer/id #uuid "be7b681a-d4b7-5605-ba2e-22acaaed6363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397301} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0d3cd9-78d0-5801-b268-5ffd73ca96c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6-{e}^{-{x}^{2}/12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397302} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c6d291-cf90-51ae-b2e3-e5400146213f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397297} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e829349-3bd7-53b1-81eb-45b5da9870a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{12x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397304} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaeb121-e0a6-501b-9a51-af6c7d17b5d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397305} {:answer/id #uuid "831b7918-1f4b-50de-b1bd-28a0091624cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397306} {:answer/id #uuid "327e9b36-504a-55cd-bdeb-9a295dbac872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397307} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1cc20f5-acca-5b79-8f90-bd4ac033d4c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b18cb65d-b781-5075-91bf-6c656ac3ed30" :question/math-academy-id "q-339145" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{6}y=x,\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $Q(x)=x.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (x/6)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}/12}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{6}y\\right) & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\cdot x \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{6}{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\cdot y & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}/12} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}) & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}/12})\\text{d}x & =\\int x{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/12} & =\\int 6{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/12} & =6{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/12} & =6{e}^{{x}^{2}/12}+C \\\\ y & =6+C{e}^{-{x}^{2}/12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=5$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =6+C{e}^{-{0}^{2}/12} \\\\ 5 & =6+C \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=6-{e}^{-{x}^{2}/12}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8748773f-b48a-58ec-b951-7e2f13629f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397420} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3eeb27-01e0-5659-a89f-03c69d0adcb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397421} {:answer/id #uuid "c5615bd6-2ce4-5da1-a40b-3086abdcadd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397422} {:answer/id #uuid "07510649-4bdf-56eb-8d9c-ed267705e564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397423} {:answer/id #uuid "03be0b57-cf22-512e-a317-32a2b0abe9aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397424} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a9dc62-5426-5219-b618-40a8ef37dfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397422} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b23de8e-d2ee-5400-a7e2-b5cd4049a1e7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "603a374f-5ef4-515c-9c7e-fa8563eb145a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397427} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb3a316-1e8b-5c4e-88ed-2cc1c3a89e42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397428} {:answer/id #uuid "e2451042-dca2-5c68-9e00-de451ff94150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397429} {:answer/id #uuid "aeaacf6b-f6f1-598e-9735-a55707c901f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397430} {:answer/id #uuid "54e98588-ab60-5ad4-b62c-cc9eebe7a31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397430} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc891073-56d2-5c57-9375-2988eb59d895" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397426} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44414e5e-ea6c-5e29-8c97-658e69445da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397433} {:answer/id #uuid "4e818916-b8da-55ed-9e45-159ab74b3b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397434} {:answer/id #uuid "fef36346-1430-5b7e-8f8d-dbd4ef5452d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397435} {:answer/id #uuid "681611df-aafb-58e2-b2fd-60ed1c5a8c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397436} {:answer/id #uuid "dca82b61-b202-5c4b-8b2a-4f67990862a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397437}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397437} :answer-field/id #uuid "d98a60af-8b0a-5156-82e3-3b9f66208118" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "df849e8a-df1b-5b2f-bbc1-5b6e2ad9aeb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-340638" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}+5x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{6}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\right\\rangle  \\\\ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\right\\rangle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{6}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\right]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{24}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-12\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & ={a}_{6}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{6} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{6} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}+5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5211e103-bbae-56b5-ab1d-86eeee675e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{4}-7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397440} {:answer/id #uuid "549261c0-0dda-5beb-b854-9f2f8b637398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{4}-7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397441} {:answer/id #uuid "eeadf2f7-53bd-5a77-9e67-80289734e565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{4}-7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397442} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c627de-a8e3-50ae-9a5e-cacf82786e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{4}-7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397443} {:answer/id #uuid "6787651e-aa4d-5093-84e5-d0a95f255245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{4}-7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397444} {:answer/id #uuid "f34b9fe5-933e-5b07-9848-aa70bc7a0b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{4}-7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397443} :answer-field/id #uuid "4484c5be-bfb9-56e1-91bb-68d4eb55bee1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397439} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "676fc215-8323-51e6-b8c4-0af2d1a3be4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397447} {:answer/id #uuid "2a944a6e-5a73-58dd-9364-1a3d02c8c7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397448} {:answer/id #uuid "c916f828-527d-56b1-a7f4-05bfb34ceabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397449} {:answer/id #uuid "64e9ee42-0c88-535d-bada-848d67fb20fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397450} {:answer/id #uuid "03257e03-c6ac-58ac-bb14-ad066e5d197b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397451}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397449} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c16f558-7e63-5623-83b7-64f167dc11d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2023ddfa-d889-50c9-878f-85d10c84ea35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397453} {:answer/id #uuid "50c89892-3614-52d6-882b-bc4708a1c3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397454} {:answer/id #uuid "ac65746c-c51f-545e-b99a-4117e9bf4102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397455} {:answer/id #uuid "b5530228-de94-5b2c-b5a7-3602545b76b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397456} {:answer/id #uuid "57843cbf-6acc-57b7-b1e7-b524e63e1320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397456} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1d13c1c-619d-5f29-9d08-2c83813e46e8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13f82e1b-2f3e-536a-8bb2-1fc6ddf6b76a" :question/math-academy-id "q-340645" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{4}-7x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{3}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\right\\rangle  \\\\ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\right\\rangle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\right]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={a}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{4}-7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6154e534-d5aa-500e-a111-0642cb1f5138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397460} {:answer/id #uuid "90e7fe06-494b-5336-8647-cec5893e79b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397461} {:answer/id #uuid "62aa5026-98f2-56d7-9078-cf1f425f67e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397462} {:answer/id #uuid "309debb7-997b-556d-8512-eacab65daa9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397463} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cdc23e-3f2e-58ea-8f36-763705ca4acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397464} {:answer/id #uuid "0da6a2a1-bbda-52c4-af89-8ae0dc014a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397465}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397460} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fe74c7f-d29a-5023-a627-6b99c33c73b4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397459} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c12b77e-d15a-5d68-b82e-75459493d093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397467} {:answer/id #uuid "375df8b6-49b7-502b-a948-207f0f617763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397468} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e087e4-24d7-5ffa-92ca-31063530bd9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397469} {:answer/id #uuid "e24a7d49-39e9-59b8-a286-404d727d74eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397470} {:answer/id #uuid "8608c77e-449a-5d7d-bd75-65fa63256437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397471}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397471} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dfa2d25-b9ae-5bb9-b792-43292f1be3e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "42bf59e6-fc11-5187-a3f2-889c117ed67e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340655" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)=\\frac{{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{s-3}+\\frac{1}{s+3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{(s+3)+(s-3)}{(s-3)(s+3)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-9}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty )\\cap (-3,\\infty )=(3,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8433c533-4904-5d92-885f-bbcd959dd940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397474} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4020d2-476a-5794-a6f2-3d8b125330ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397474} :answer-field/id #uuid "199c37d6-b74a-50da-a86e-fce04acf3561" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397473} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "deda8e08-d6bd-5e0e-ae0c-00112e17523f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397477} {:answer/id #uuid "4d231c1e-d79b-5508-b44e-84b13e3b138c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397478} {:answer/id #uuid "2134b48b-4f80-54ce-97ca-e2ded504ec18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397479} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbe5295-15de-5429-ac7d-1288d0b2be34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397479} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ea71ffc-f171-5611-bfc4-d57724eb7008" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3d6ca311-b229-51f5-b6ee-b805697465fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340669" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\right]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-6\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $g(x)=1$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d80295e-b463-5e7c-bf1a-e7d0d9f86058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397490} {:answer/id #uuid "08ab9610-98a7-5832-b2b7-84ed96b7fb63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397491} {:answer/id #uuid "660727ef-7b2d-531b-8658-07e77f29dd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397492} :answer-field/id #uuid "3811f53b-a1af-5f49-ab19-7ecfb660367f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397489} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e77341d6-4bd5-508f-b10c-e6b1abd62aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{5{e}^{-5s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397494} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd85fe4-dc49-5e10-8a3c-4aae5e0e97e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397495} {:answer/id #uuid "5a60055f-03ee-5493-96d8-f946458ffa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397496} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6b9f59-ce56-5db4-9460-84a4cfcd9c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{5{e}^{-5s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397497} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7ef87a-673e-59b2-8d9c-a72764dc0faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397498}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397498} :answer-field/id #uuid "3311ff79-3ee4-524b-acee-81bb9dd688cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "94c1a445-854c-59cd-8bb7-f1e99e5361c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-340883" :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ for $f(t)=u(t-2)+u(t-5),$ where $u$ is the unit step function.$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}},\nvalid for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise functions $u(t-2)$ and $u(t-5),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2) & =\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 2\\end{cases} \\\\ u(t-5) & =\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<5 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 5.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nu(t-2)+u(t-5)=\\begin{cases}0,\\, & t<2 \\\\ 1,\\, & 2\\le t<5 \\\\ 2,\\, & t\\ge 5.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSo, for our Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{2}^{5}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the second integral diverges for $s\\le 0.$\n\nFor $s>0,$ we calculate our Laplace transform as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{5}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\left[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right]}_{2}^{5}+2\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}\\right)|}_{5}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({e}^{-sa}-{e}^{-5s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}(0-{e}^{-5s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-5s}}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s}+\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s},\\,s>0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a145f15c-ceb4-50f6-84e5-8b7834619199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397513} {:answer/id #uuid "29c9ba6e-cb4a-511a-a3e8-afa19fa0cd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397514} {:answer/id #uuid "a538529b-1b93-5744-80c9-65509cce8a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397515} {:answer/id #uuid "651aadd0-1e95-54cb-96ff-254e6eef8952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397516} {:answer/id #uuid "871991e0-f1d8-558e-90c5-5f962efd70eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397517} {:answer/id #uuid "3aaeac27-3d73-5c9c-86eb-0838799cd60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397518}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397517} :answer-field/id #uuid "4550c540-2a81-570b-87fb-628f14326571" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397512} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee99e0c-d4c4-5917-b998-b7dec7f37dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397520} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb3ed42-d158-5ad8-af7f-d31ff6b6b3c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397521} {:answer/id #uuid "4a13a688-1c5e-567d-bb42-ea175ded90cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397522} {:answer/id #uuid "7aac7682-f32b-5b3d-b4d4-32d39a154d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397523} {:answer/id #uuid "88396452-9be4-582b-b646-fd6f295eadb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397524} :answer-field/id #uuid "b87144df-013d-5d61-8a79-c97d52888b76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "db14dea0-146d-5df5-a79a-6c50bf00be55" :question/math-academy-id "q-341136" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the cosine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{(s+4\\text{i})+(s-4\\text{i})}{(s-4\\text{i})(s+4\\text{i})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+16}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "461b0ac0-5211-5e64-aa63-d08093e5e804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397527} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b6b192-ec4b-5aca-a7e5-b7e2362c242a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8s}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397528} {:answer/id #uuid "992044c1-c091-557f-b232-73069b3560a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397529} {:answer/id #uuid "2e13c630-4b5b-5b09-ab98-ae7663e336b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397530} {:answer/id #uuid "e26f2812-efee-58e1-bc49-fa54b5fa6464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397531} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e065cee-e495-59be-a49b-48b465530e20" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397526} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "758b98a0-94c0-5be6-b097-b85d31f91123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397533} {:answer/id #uuid "91240e34-abfd-5826-835b-a95cbbd93c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397534} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c6fc60-0e73-5a81-8093-d5c8cfec9a2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge -8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397535} {:answer/id #uuid "93d7be6d-9a7a-59df-b1e3-a5deb3df60a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397536} {:answer/id #uuid "3942e53b-efa0-5e2e-aeb2-41186bd71234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397537} {:answer/id #uuid "9f65ca28-dfe3-5a9c-8051-5e8c9de202af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397538}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397534} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d76374-c302-5673-ab25-1a0eb5568341" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f89dc302-60ce-5bd9-b567-660590ce563f" :question/math-academy-id "q-341140" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)=\\frac{{e}^{8t}-{e}^{-8t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}-{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}}{2}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{8t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-8t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{8t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-8},\\,s>8 \\\\ L{{e}^{-8t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+8},\\,s>-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{8t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-8t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-8}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{s-8}-\\frac{1}{s+8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{(s+8)-(s-8)}{(s-8)(s+8)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{16}{{s}^{2}-64}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{8t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (8,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-8t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-8,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(8,\\infty )\\cap (-8,\\infty )=(8,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7d31a3-b2e8-5955-b7a5-8d0265e34185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397541} {:answer/id #uuid "14f752d4-26f3-5b55-951a-267514a31272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397542} {:answer/id #uuid "86ecc11d-d361-56e3-b985-f0af7df41e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397543} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecabd1c-c6be-5ae6-a083-97bdd6237c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397544} {:answer/id #uuid "b23326b9-dc50-5189-864f-580f0beb25f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397545}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397545} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e8826c-c720-5c71-8e60-9efc9028ee9f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397540} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b0c5c0-5b9c-5ca0-a3b8-c0e23762591f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397547} {:answer/id #uuid "037567f7-c39a-5a21-9477-5930025ede7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397548} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2e6cae-f02e-5650-9bdf-362d1f1fa2b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397549} {:answer/id #uuid "64699d60-b695-59b3-84f5-62b93bdee4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397550} {:answer/id #uuid "4c59d8f0-ecad-5bf2-9285-f977292bfdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397551} :answer-field/id #uuid "df14591b-b6cc-5cdd-adc7-9dbfe95edc2a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397546} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd7a6256-c073-5d27-93a0-6184aec661c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397553} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d09424-4779-58cb-a9a8-a20b503a58f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397554} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0b285c-99a5-5dc6-b53e-349ca170a4bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397555} {:answer/id #uuid "d6de7f97-87a8-587c-be9e-52648a5b0102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397556} {:answer/id #uuid "330f62f3-2b9c-5888-9cec-d52701d82d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨1,1⟩}{⟨f(x),1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397557}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397555} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e635cb7-5fb0-5dc2-9345-111ede6d51d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "73b220ad-78ed-5227-b775-ed2065f7b0fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-341308" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=3x+1$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{0}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),1\\right\\rangle  \\\\ &= \\left\\langle \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},1\\right\\rangle +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{-0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩} \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397600 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488da8c0-0995-59d5-a5c6-54b8e67769d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s-4}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397602} {:answer/id #uuid "a55e62b5-e47d-5336-b8b7-276e9c1dbb68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s+4}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397603} {:answer/id #uuid "f47e74e2-4bce-5a44-817d-59bd1c489c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397604} {:answer/id #uuid "02cd391d-9944-5308-bbce-ea584369dba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397605} {:answer/id #uuid "79c67eea-4e83-584e-9efc-0ea95542dae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397606} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c237e54-7764-534a-9c54-8baed4a37d20" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2225ade3-5bf4-52e8-bb8f-84401c8e6e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s+2}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397608} {:answer/id #uuid "9a61bdd8-3d2f-572c-91f5-833d4e189e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10(s+2)}-\\frac{3s+4}{10({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397609} {:answer/id #uuid "4d73190f-40e0-562b-8571-039c42b83caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397610} {:answer/id #uuid "d6087e3e-f080-5ae6-bb69-2acbb87ff902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10(s+2)}+\\frac{3s+4}{10({s}^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397611}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397609} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f001e5-aaed-5c9c-a1ac-1f1aa18ef7db" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c20f35-2312-5896-8f00-c76ef490da78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397613} {:answer/id #uuid "287b1162-26e2-52bb-99aa-815c269f399d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397614} {:answer/id #uuid "a3aed227-d2f9-59e8-8313-76d8cc49a807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397615} {:answer/id #uuid "7e56b7cb-60e3-5844-8de1-228ec8842538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397615} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e35ac8d-bb69-5204-8ed0-7880d0ca6330" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1873e66d-b721-525d-8ac0-7b13c3d92c58" :question/math-academy-id "q-342960" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+2L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}-L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}=\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{4-s}{{s}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{4-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+16),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{4-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+16)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n4-s=A({s}^{2}+16)+(Bs+C)(s+2)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n4-(-2)=A((-2)^{2}+16)+0\\,⟹\\,6=20A\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=\\frac{3}{10}+B\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n4=16A+2C\\,⟹\\,4=\\frac{48}{10}+2C\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{3}{10(s+2)}+\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{3}{10}s-\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}{{s}^{2}+16}=\\frac{3}{10(s+2)}-\\frac{3s+4}{10({s}^{2}+16)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}} & ={e}^{-2t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =\\frac{3}{10}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}-\\frac{3}{10}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}}-\\frac{1}{10}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\frac{3}{10}{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af7b840f-2e0c-52c0-b79f-c78277457040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{54}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397664} {:answer/id #uuid "addf1d04-69f0-5452-bba4-8900e12fa907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397665} {:answer/id #uuid "8626a439-85ec-5b4a-9bb1-b4485f902170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397666}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397666} :answer-field/id #uuid "865ba785-fbd6-5a07-957c-16cb2c1df477" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397663} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a53b81f-d126-598c-8b37-6e225ea3cb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397668} {:answer/id #uuid "f5abb3ad-0dba-5b32-98f6-df1e57e1d85e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397669} {:answer/id #uuid "0102c81a-3dd5-541b-9257-ca77f5322bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397670} :answer-field/id #uuid "91f55656-129c-5fe5-8079-32fba851d88e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22623b70-5aab-52fc-9402-941db3c703fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397672} {:answer/id #uuid "ee445dc1-2657-5591-8683-e95785717f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397673} {:answer/id #uuid "d51d9118-2abe-5ef4-9ada-c8a08d3b5d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397674} {:answer/id #uuid "d01d3c08-0c36-57bc-8823-3c469f701c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397672} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d625df2-64a2-5d5b-b5dc-aa82f78979a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15f5a0b9-1297-5188-a047-103209518fed" :question/math-academy-id "q-343855" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & \\,-3\\left(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n} &=\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=\\frac{4}{3},$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, ${b}_{n}=\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3\\left(\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }\\right)=\\frac{6}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{9{a}_{n}}{n\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼\\frac{2}{3} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{18}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{162}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5beaf63-a24e-5096-a60c-b0469d5c3b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397678} {:answer/id #uuid "abc5583a-c2d4-5270-9535-e8dc76611c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397679} {:answer/id #uuid "20b43c86-9df5-5ea5-b8db-701f1d2fcb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397680} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b221ad-c466-515a-9758-208b0b79820b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397679} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfa212b9-4a9e-5ed1-977d-8a0f92f187bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397677} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c55a33cd-7217-5f5d-aae1-44a553c062e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397683} {:answer/id #uuid "68fd5497-22fd-5e95-baa6-a3fb2a651897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{30(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397684} {:answer/id #uuid "9261cac5-a1b2-5b9e-85af-f8f060b21c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397685}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397683} :answer-field/id #uuid "685da459-566a-5cb9-907e-c163e95f44e3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397682} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52840280-13ca-54da-b4d4-afd74b42c883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397687} {:answer/id #uuid "7120f89a-088f-5258-8c2d-358cc96f6e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397688} {:answer/id #uuid "15b8dddf-399a-5ce8-a3a7-717ad28083fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397688} :answer-field/id #uuid "615c3feb-87ab-595d-bd3c-9d3dd60c0bff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b2c46dfd-7116-546d-b775-da0e3f310626" :question/math-academy-id "q-343860" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right)+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\left({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & \\,-5\\left(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-5 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n} &=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n} &=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, ${b}_{n}=\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5\\left(\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }\\right)=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225} \\\\  & =\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26abab5a-e0d3-5a63-b7b9-8c8b067c042b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397692} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc27d3d-2228-5745-9f75-6ad665b3203a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397693} {:answer/id #uuid "c26489de-65c7-593c-9bc1-121a32b98a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397694} {:answer/id #uuid "efa4633c-d9b6-5177-bd10-dd0e9bfa3eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397695} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d3c5e72-c342-555b-a87c-e14e208988df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc221971-12de-52c7-8478-0068ddd7a170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{54\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397697} {:answer/id #uuid "fe05ab27-ffe9-519c-a245-1e3105109d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397698} {:answer/id #uuid "96ad6016-80ff-5081-b817-514fd1499c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397699} {:answer/id #uuid "0e910e33-e3b2-5731-a6d5-5a16e1e9ad14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{216\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397700}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397699} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa9bba7-0c1d-559d-9dd6-0cd8ed6cec73" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d694cb78-fd17-5d72-bd5e-fb91875a20a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343891" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $1$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{36{\\pi }^{2}}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $\\left(L=\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=6\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))+x(-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+36{\\pi }^{2}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)-36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+36{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-12\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x)+12\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12\\pi A &=0 \\\\ 12\\pi B &=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{1}{108\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{108\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ed958e4-1f96-524f-b3de-110ff91c56eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397771} {:answer/id #uuid "2965ac18-e5e8-50d6-a1d8-51c095517414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397772} {:answer/id #uuid "1716108b-afba-5b8e-8f20-72faa3deabf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397773} {:answer/id #uuid "352ae16b-e108-578f-a46d-867b64df0c48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397773} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f88564-b8cd-591d-a785-13a83fdfb6b9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c4364f-4fa5-52be-b64d-080c7d0e12da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397776} {:answer/id #uuid "52293ede-63c0-5c50-a1e8-a410e4858c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397777} {:answer/id #uuid "e32c59ab-db42-5218-85ae-4fb5278cdf4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397778} {:answer/id #uuid "7790bcee-04c3-594b-bb1b-33e4a4b043f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397776} :answer-field/id #uuid "b539044b-f263-566b-a583-7f00bd13bba1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "01283c40-bda7-59b4-8f16-b320c423d254" :question/math-academy-id "q-365387" :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(x\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(t) \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{x}{{t}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-\\frac{1}{t} & =-\\frac{x}{{t}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{t} & =\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t({t}^{2}-1)}{{t}^{2}-x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t({t}^{2}-1)}{{t}^{2}-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{t({t}^{2}-1)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{t}^{2}-x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} \\\\ t({t}^{2}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={t}^{2}-x \\\\ t({t}^{2}-1)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x & ={t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t({t}^{2}-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{x}{t({t}^{2}-1)} & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1}{t({t}^{2}-1)},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}-1}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec330cc-8c77-5e4b-b2b9-ffed05aa242e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397789} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0961d6-77ee-5ef6-b56f-f97f7519ca2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397790} {:answer/id #uuid "67f68e51-f432-57bf-9c04-5cee503b7449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397791} {:answer/id #uuid "297fec68-7633-5a4f-b758-461f512bfa1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397792} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7e14f3-bd1f-5912-9586-f5fdac2f41f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397793}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397791} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdede32f-8b30-513b-878d-48ae1f1b992d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d8d217e5-8474-5d6b-93f2-6f6e1253c6f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-103991" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)}\\,\\text{d}x=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+C,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)} & =\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x+6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n7=A(x+6)+B(x-1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(-6+6)+B(-6-1) \\\\ 7 & =-7B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(1+6)+B(1-1) \\\\ 7 & =7A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)} & =\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{(x-1)(x+6)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+6}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+6|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,b=-1,$ and $a+b=1-1=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232441c2-f7f1-5f16-bc4b-60b09c0b1593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397838} {:answer/id #uuid "82fba5c9-0131-541c-8948-a25c0d8eae2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397839} {:answer/id #uuid "ef45acfd-4144-56f2-98cf-f241f28d614f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi \\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397840} {:answer/id #uuid "0f87d6d3-3c3b-5273-849d-9b1476a53757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397841} {:answer/id #uuid "c5222c9f-23b7-5ec8-b93f-57e76ab64107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397840} :answer-field/id #uuid "dad99554-fa4d-57b5-9d6d-0148f288e880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da600baa-a9d3-5724-9a5b-8920df8b8600" :question/math-academy-id "q-106293" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d698646baac96e15db7f447c0e1af6dbc0cb03cae50084fbfbb6cab15eabf565.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=2.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/b7/b76043921a3f9399049d6c3b4f557c7263da4d38a070c66a2dd444dbe2806c8e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $\\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi \\right]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in \\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi \\right].\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "232441c2-f7f1-5f16-bc4b-60b09c0b1593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397838} {:answer/id #uuid "82fba5c9-0131-541c-8948-a25c0d8eae2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397839} {:answer/id #uuid "ef45acfd-4144-56f2-98cf-f241f28d614f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi \\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397840}]
